-- V472: Seed 132 Class 11 Physics Mock Tests (104 Real-Format 70M Papers + 28 Chapter-Wise 25M Mocks)

DO $$
DECLARE
    v_class_id UUID;
BEGIN
    SELECT id INTO v_class_id FROM learn_exam_types WHERE slug IN ('class-11', 'cbse-class-11') LIMIT 1;
    IF v_class_id IS NULL THEN
        SELECT id INTO v_class_id FROM learn_exam_types WHERE name ILIKE '%Class 11%' LIMIT 1;
    END IF;

    IF v_class_id IS NOT NULL THEN
        DELETE FROM learn_test_attempts WHERE mock_test_id = 'a1111111-0000-0000-0000-000000000011';
        DELETE FROM learn_test_questions WHERE mock_test_id = 'a1111111-0000-0000-0000-000000000011';
        DELETE FROM learn_mock_tests WHERE id = 'a1111111-0000-0000-0000-000000000011';

        DELETE FROM learn_test_questions WHERE mock_test_id IN (SELECT id FROM learn_mock_tests WHERE id::text LIKE '11010000-%');
        DELETE FROM learn_mock_tests WHERE id::text LIKE '11010000-%';

        ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

Reason (R): Dimensionless constants like 1/2 or 2π cannot be determined by dimensional analysis.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Physics', 'Units and Measurements', 1, 'Dimensional analysis cannot detect pure numerical factors.', NULL, NULL, 'Dimensional analysis cannot detect pure numerical factors.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000007', 'Find the value of 60 J/min on a system that has 100 g, 100 cm, and 1 min as base units.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Units and Measurements', 2, 'Power = 60 J / 60 s = 1 W = 1 kg m² s⁻³. n₂ = 1 × (1000/100)¹ × (100/100)² × (1/60)⁻³ = 10 × 1 × 216000 = 2.16 × 10⁶ new units.', 'Unit conversion formula (1M) + 2.16×10⁶ (1M)', NULL, 'Power = 60 J / 60 s = 1 W = 1 kg m² s⁻³. n₂ = 1 × (1000/100)¹ × (100/100)² × (1/60)⁻³ = 10 × 1 × 216000 = 2.16 × 10⁶ new units.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000008', 'Check the dimensional correctness of v² - u² = 2as.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Units and Measurements', 2, 'LHS: [LT⁻¹]² = [L²T⁻²]. RHS: [LT⁻²][L] = [L²T⁻²]. Both sides identical, hence dimensionally correct.', 'LHS & RHS dimensions (2M)', NULL, 'LHS: [LT⁻¹]² = [L²T⁻²]. RHS: [LT⁻²][L] = [L²T⁻²]. Both sides identical, hence dimensionally correct.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000009', 'The time period T of a simple pendulum depends on mass m, length l, and acceleration due to gravity g. Derive the formula using dimensional analysis.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Units and Measurements', 3, 'T = k m^a l^b g^c -> [T] = [M^a L^(b+c) T^(-2c)] => a=0, -2c=1 => c=-1/2, b=1/2 -> T = k √(l/g).', 'Equating powers (2M) + T = k√(l/g) (1M)', NULL, 'T = k m^a l^b g^c -> [T] = [M^a L^(b+c) T^(-2c)] => a=0, -2c=1 => c=-1/2, b=1/2 -> T = k √(l/g).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000a', 'State three limitations of dimensional analysis with examples.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Units and Measurements', 3, '(1) Cannot determine dimensionless constants (k = 2π). (2) Fails for trigonometric, exponential, or logarithmic terms. (3) Fails when a quantity depends on more than 3 independent base variables in mechanics or involves sum/difference of terms.', '3 × 1M', NULL, '(1) Cannot determine dimensionless constants (k = 2π). (2) Fails for trigonometric, exponential, or logarithmic terms. (3) Fails when a quantity depends on more than 3 independent base variables in mechanics or involves sum/difference of terms.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000b', 'State the Principle of Homogeneity of dimensions. Apply it to: (a) find dimensions of constants a and b in van der Waals equation (P + a/V²)(V - b) = RT, and (b) find dimensions of α and β in F = αt + βt² + γ/x.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Physics', 'Units and Measurements', 5, '(a) [a/V²]=[P] => [a]=[ML⁻¹T⁻²][L⁶] = [ML⁵T⁻²]; [b]=[V]=[L³]. (b) [α]=[F/t]=[MLT⁻³]; [β]=[F/t²]=[MLT⁻⁴].', 'Principle (1M) + van der Waals (2M) + Force equation (2M)', NULL, '(a) [a/V²]=[P] => [a]=[ML⁻¹T⁻²][L⁶] = [ML⁵T⁻²]; [b]=[V]=[L³]. (b) [α]=[F/t]=[MLT⁻³]; [β]=[F/t²]=[MLT⁻⁴].', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000c', '(i) Round off 3.745 and 3.735 to 3 significant figures. (ii) Add 7.213 g, 16.5 g, and 0.42 g to proper significant figures. (iii) Why is 4.700 m more precise than 4.7 m? (iv) Write dimensional formula of Universal Gravitational Constant G.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Physics', 'Units and Measurements', 4, '(i) 3.74 and 3.74 (even digit rule). (ii) Sum = 24.133 -> rounded to 1 decimal place = 24.1 g. (iii) Smaller least count (0.001 m vs 0.1 m). (iv) [M⁻¹L³T⁻²].', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'During a vernier callipers lab, students compare instrument least counts and apply rounding rules to record density measurements without false precision.', '(i) 3.74 and 3.74 (even digit rule). (ii) Sum = 24.133 -> rounded to 1 decimal place = 24.1 g. (iii) Smaller least count (0.001 m vs 0.1 m). (iv) [M⁻¹L³T⁻²].', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000d', 'Slope of velocity-time graph gives:', '{"A":"Displacement","B":"Instantaneous acceleration","C":"Average speed","D":"Jerk"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Straight Line', 1, 'a = dv/dt = slope of v-t graph.', NULL, NULL, 'a = dv/dt = slope of v-t graph.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000e', 'Area enclosed under a velocity-time graph represents:', '{"A":"Acceleration","B":"Displacement","C":"Distance only","D":"Impulse"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Straight Line', 1, '∫v dt = displacement.', NULL, NULL, '∫v dt = displacement.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000000f', 'A ball thrown vertically upward returns to thrower after 6 s. Velocity of projection (g = 10 m/s²) is:', '{"A":"15 m/s","B":"30 m/s","C":"60 m/s","D":"20 m/s"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Straight Line', 1, 'Time of ascent = 3 s => u = gt = 30 m/s.', NULL, NULL, 'Time of ascent = 3 s => u = gt = 30 m/s.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000010', 'A body covers half distance at speed v₁ and remaining half at v₂. Average speed is:', '{"A":"(v₁+v₂)/2","B":"2v₁v₂/(v₁+v₂)","C":"√(v₁v₂)","D":"(v₁+v₂)/v₁v₂"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Straight Line', 1, 'Harmonic mean 2v₁v₂/(v₁+v₂).', NULL, NULL, 'Harmonic mean 2v₁v₂/(v₁+v₂).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000011', 'If x = 2t³ - 6t² + 5, acceleration at t = 2 s is:', '{"A":"12 m/s²","B":"24 m/s²","C":"6 m/s²","D":"0"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Straight Line', 1, 'v = 6t²-12t; a = 12t - 12 => at t=2, a = 12 m/s².', NULL, NULL, 'v = 6t²-12t; a = 12t - 12 => at t=2, a = 12 m/s².', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000012', 'Assertion (A): An object can have zero velocity yet non-zero acceleration at an instant.
Reason (R): At the highest point of vertical motion under gravity, velocity is zero while acceleration equals g downward.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Straight Line', 1, 'Instantaneous v=0 does not imply a=0.', NULL, NULL, 'Instantaneous v=0 does not imply a=0.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000013', 'Distinguish between distance and displacement with one example where displacement is zero but distance is positive.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Straight Line', 2, 'Distance is scalar path length; displacement is shortest vector from initial to final position. Completing one full circular lap gives zero displacement and 2πR distance.', '1M difference + 1M example', NULL, 'Distance is scalar path length; displacement is shortest vector from initial to final position. Completing one full circular lap gives zero displacement and 2πR distance.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000014', 'A car moving at 72 km/h stops in 5 s after applying brakes. Calculate retardation and stopping distance.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Straight Line', 2, 'u = 20 m/s. a = -20/5 = -4 m/s² (retardation 4 m/s²). s = ½(20+0)×5 = 50 m.', '1M each', NULL, 'u = 20 m/s. a = -20/5 = -4 m/s² (retardation 4 m/s²). s = ½(20+0)×5 = 50 m.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000015', 'Derive the second equation of kinematics s = ut + ½at² using a velocity-time graph.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Straight Line', 3, 'Trapezium area under straight-line v-t from u to v in time t = rectangle ut + triangle ½(v-u)t; substitute v-u = at -> s = ut + ½at².', 'Graph (1M) + Area derivation (2M)', NULL, 'Trapezium area under straight-line v-t from u to v in time t = rectangle ut + triangle ½(v-u)t; substitute v-u = at -> s = ut + ½at².', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000016', 'A body falls freely from rest under gravity. Find the ratio of distances covered in 1st, 2nd, and 3rd seconds of fall.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Straight Line', 3, 's_n = u + a/2 (2n - 1) = (g/2)(2n - 1). Ratio s₁ : s₂ : s₃ = 1 : 3 : 5 (Galileo''s law of odd numbers).', 'Formula (1M) + 1:3:5 derivation (2M)', NULL, 's_n = u + a/2 (2n - 1) = (g/2)(2n - 1). Ratio s₁ : s₂ : s₃ = 1 : 3 : 5 (Galileo''s law of odd numbers).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000017', 'Using calculus method, derive all three equations of uniformly accelerated motion: (i) v = u + at, (ii) s = ut + ½at², (iii) v² - u² = 2as. Also prove s_n = u + a/2(2n - 1).', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Straight Line', 5, 'Integrate dv = a dt (1.5M), dx = (u+at)dt (1.5M), v dv = a dx (1.5M), s_n = s(n) - s(n-1) (0.5M).', 'Three calculus derivations + nth second distance (5M)', NULL, 'Integrate dv = a dt (1.5M), dx = (u+at)dt (1.5M), v dv = a dx (1.5M), s_n = s(n) - s(n-1) (0.5M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000018', '(i) Find position at t=0 and t=2s for x = 8.5 + 2.5t². (ii) Average velocity between t=2s and t=4s. (iii) Instantaneous velocity at t=3s. (iv) Is motion uniformly accelerated?', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Straight Line', 4, '(i) 8.5 m and 18.5 m. (ii) [x(4)-x(2)]/2 = (48.5-18.5)/2 = 15 m/s. (iii) v = dx/dt = 5t -> at t=3s, 15 m/s. (iv) Yes, a = dv/dt = 5 m/s² (constant).', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'A toy train moves along a straight rail such that its coordinate in metres varies with time t (seconds) as x(t) = 8.5 + 2.5t².', '(i) 8.5 m and 18.5 m. (ii) [x(4)-x(2)]/2 = (48.5-18.5)/2 = 15 m/s. (iii) v = dx/dt = 5t -> at t=3s, 15 m/s. (iv) Yes, a = dv/dt = 5 m/s² (constant).', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000019', 'Angle between two equal vectors A and A when their resultant also equals A is:', '{"A":"60°","B":"90°","C":"120°","D":"180°"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Plane', 1, 'A² + A² + 2A²cosθ = A² => cosθ = -1/2 => θ = 120°.', NULL, NULL, 'A² + A² + 2A²cosθ = A² => cosθ = -1/2 => θ = 120°.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001a', 'Maximum horizontal range for projectile speed u is achieved at launch angle:', '{"A":"30°","B":"45°","C":"60°","D":"90°"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Plane', 1, 'R = u²sin(2θ)/g is maximum at 2θ=90° => θ=45°.', NULL, NULL, 'R = u²sin(2θ)/g is maximum at 2θ=90° => θ=45°.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001b', 'Centripetal acceleration of a particle moving in a circle of radius R with constant speed v is:', '{"A":"v/R","B":"v²/R directed radially inward","C":"v²R","D":"Zero"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Plane', 1, 'a_c = v²/R = ω²R toward centre.', NULL, NULL, 'a_c = v²/R = ω²R toward centre.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001c', 'For a projectile, at the highest point of its trajectory, angle between velocity and acceleration is:', '{"A":"0°","B":"45°","C":"90°","D":"180°"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Plane', 1, 'Velocity is purely horizontal (u cosθ); g is vertically downward -> 90°.', NULL, NULL, 'Velocity is purely horizontal (u cosθ); g is vertically downward -> 90°.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001d', 'Two projectiles thrown at angles 30° and 60° with identical speeds have equal:', '{"A":"Maximum heights","B":"Time of flight","C":"Horizontal ranges","D":"Final vertical velocities"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Plane', 1, 'Complementary angles θ and 90°-θ yield identical horizontal range.', NULL, NULL, 'Complementary angles θ and 90°-θ yield identical horizontal range.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001e', 'Assertion (A): Uniform circular motion is an accelerated motion even though speed remains constant.
Reason (R): Direction of velocity continuously changes at every point along the circular tangent.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Plane', 1, 'Velocity vector changes direction -> centripetal acceleration.', NULL, NULL, 'Velocity vector changes direction -> centripetal acceleration.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000001f', 'If |A + B| = |A - B|, find the angle between vectors A and B.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Plane', 2, 'A²+B²+2ABcosθ = A²+B²-2ABcosθ => 4ABcosθ = 0 => θ = 90°.', 'Squaring (1M) + θ=90° (1M)', NULL, 'A²+B²+2ABcosθ = A²+B²-2ABcosθ => 4ABcosθ = 0 => θ = 90°.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000020', 'Find the unit vector along A = 3î + 4ĵ.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Plane', 2, '|A| = √(9+16) = 5. Â = (3î + 4ĵ)/5 = 0.6î + 0.8ĵ.', 'Magnitude 5 (1M) + Unit vector (1M)', NULL, '|A| = √(9+16) = 5. Â = (3î + 4ĵ)/5 = 0.6î + 0.8ĵ.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000021', 'Prove that the trajectory of a projectile launched at angle θ with speed u is a parabola.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Plane', 3, 'x = (u cosθ)t => t = x/(u cosθ). y = (u sinθ)t - ½gt² = x tanθ - [g/(2u²cos²θ)]x², which matches y = ax - bx² (parabola).', 'x, y equations (1.5M) + Parabolic equation (1.5M)', NULL, 'x = (u cosθ)t => t = x/(u cosθ). y = (u sinθ)t - ½gt² = x tanθ - [g/(2u²cos²θ)]x², which matches y = ax - bx² (parabola).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000022', 'Derive the expressions for Time of Flight (T) and Maximum Height (H) of an oblique projectile.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Plane', 3, 'At peak v_y = 0 = u sinθ - gt_m => t_m = u sinθ/g; T = 2t_m = 2u sinθ/g. H = u²sin²θ/(2g) from v_y² = u_y² - 2gH.', 'T derivation (1.5M) + H derivation (1.5M)', NULL, 'At peak v_y = 0 = u sinθ - gt_m => t_m = u sinθ/g; T = 2t_m = 2u sinθ/g. H = u²sin²θ/(2g) from v_y² = u_y² - 2gH.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000023', 'State Parallelogram Law of Vector Addition and derive magnitude R and direction α of resultant of two vectors P and Q inclined at θ. Also find the angle of projection for which horizontal range equals maximum height.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Plane', 5, 'Resultant R = √(P²+Q²+2PQcosθ), tan α = (Q sinθ)/(P + Q cosθ) (3.5M). R = H => u²(2sinθcosθ)/g = u²sin²θ/(2g) => tanθ = 4 => θ = tan⁻¹(4) ≈ 76° (1.5M).', 'Parallelogram law & derivation (3.5M) + R=H angle (1.5M)', NULL, 'Resultant R = √(P²+Q²+2PQcosθ), tan α = (Q sinθ)/(P + Q cosθ) (3.5M). R = H => u²(2sinθcosθ)/g = u²sin²θ/(2g) => tanθ = 4 => θ = tan⁻¹(4) ≈ 76° (1.5M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000024', '(i) Direction in which swimmer should head to cross shortest path straight across river. (ii) Minimum time to cross a 400 m wide river if swimmer speed in still water is 4 m/s and river flows at 3 m/s. (iii) Drift distance in minimum-time case. (iv) Net ground speed in minimum-time crossing.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Plane', 4, '(i) Upstream at angle sinθ = v_r/v_s. (ii) t_min = 400/4 = 100 s (heading perpendicular). (iii) Drift = v_r × t = 3 × 100 = 300 m. (iv) √(4² + 3²) = 5 m/s.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'A swimmer capable of swimming at 4 m/s in still water attempts to cross a 400 m wide straight river flowing steadily at 3 m/s.', '(i) Upstream at angle sinθ = v_r/v_s. (ii) t_min = 400/4 = 100 s (heading perpendicular). (iii) Drift = v_r × t = 3 × 100 = 300 m. (iv) √(4² + 3²) = 5 m/s.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000025', 'Newton''s first law of motion defines:', '{"A":"Force qualitatively and Inertia","B":"Impulse","C":"Action-reaction","D":"Torque"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Laws of Motion', 1, 'Law of Inertia defines force and inertia.', NULL, NULL, 'Law of Inertia defines force and inertia.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000026', 'A 2 kg mass receives impulse 10 N·s from rest. Its final velocity is:', '{"A":"2 m/s","B":"5 m/s","C":"10 m/s","D":"20 m/s"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Laws of Motion', 1, 'J = Δp = m(v-0) => v = 10/2 = 5 m/s.', NULL, NULL, 'J = Δp = m(v-0) => v = 10/2 = 5 m/s.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000027', 'Maximum safe speed on a flat circular road of radius r and friction coefficient μ is:', '{"A":"√(μrg)","B":"μrg","C":"√(rg/μ)","D":"2μrg"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Laws of Motion', 1, 'mv²/r ≤ μmg => v_max = √(μrg).', NULL, NULL, 'mv²/r ≤ μmg => v_max = √(μrg).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000028', 'Rocket propulsion works on the principle of:', '{"A":"Conservation of energy only","B":"Conservation of linear momentum (Newton''s 3rd Law)","C":"Archimedes principle","D":"Pascal''s law"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Laws of Motion', 1, 'Backward exhaust momentum propels rocket forward.', NULL, NULL, 'Backward exhaust momentum propels rocket forward.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000029', 'Coefficient of static friction μ_s between two dry surfaces is generally:', '{"A":"Less than kinetic friction μ_k","B":"Slightly greater than kinetic friction μ_k","C":"Equal to 10 always","D":"Zero"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Laws of Motion', 1, 'Breaking cold-weld interlocking requires μ_s > μ_k.', NULL, NULL, 'Breaking cold-weld interlocking requires μ_s > μ_k.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002a', 'Assertion (A): A cricketer pulls his hands backward while catching a fast-moving ball.
Reason (R): Increasing the time duration of impact reduces the average impulsive force F = Δp/Δt on his palms.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Physics', 'Laws of Motion', 1, 'Longer stopping time reduces peak force.', NULL, NULL, 'Longer stopping time reduces peak force.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002b', 'Why are circular roads banked at sharp curves? Write the formula for optimum speed on a banked road without friction.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Laws of Motion', 2, 'Banking tilts the normal reaction horizontally inward to provide essential centripetal force without relying solely on tyre friction. v_0 = √(rg tanθ).', 'Reason (1M) + Formula (1M)', NULL, 'Banking tilts the normal reaction horizontally inward to provide essential centripetal force without relying solely on tyre friction. v_0 = √(rg tanθ).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002c', 'A shell of mass 0.02 kg is fired by a gun of mass 100 kg with muzzle speed 80 m/s. Find recoil speed of gun.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Laws of Motion', 2, 'M V = m v => V = (0.02 × 80)/100 = 0.016 m/s (1.6 cm/s).', 'Conservation eq (1M) + 0.016 m/s (1M)', NULL, 'M V = m v => V = (0.02 × 80)/100 = 0.016 m/s (1.6 cm/s).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002d', 'State and prove the Law of Conservation of Linear Momentum from Newton''s Second and Third Laws.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Laws of Motion', 3, 'If F_ext = 0, F_12 = -F_21 => dp₁/dt + dp₂/dt = 0 => p₁ + p₂ = constant.', 'Statements (1M) + Proof (2M)', NULL, 'If F_ext = 0, F_12 = -F_21 => dp₁/dt + dp₂/dt = 0 => p₁ + p₂ = constant.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002e', 'Explain why it is easier to pull a lawn roller than to push it with the same force at angle θ to horizontal.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Laws of Motion', 3, 'Pulling resolves upward F sinθ reducing normal reaction N = mg - F sinθ (less friction). Pushing adds downward F sinθ increasing N = mg + F sinθ (more friction).', 'Pull N (1.5M) + Push N (1.5M)', NULL, 'Pulling resolves upward F sinθ reducing normal reaction N = mg - F sinθ (less friction). Pushing adds downward F sinθ increasing N = mg + F sinθ (more friction).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000002f', 'Derive the maximum safe speed v_max of a car on a banked circular road of radius r, banking angle θ, and coefficient of friction μ. Reduce it for μ = 0.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Physics', 'Laws of Motion', 5, 'Resolve N cosθ = mg + f sinθ and N sinθ + f cosθ = mv²/r with f_max = μN. Divide equations to obtain v_max = √[rg (μ + tanθ)/(1 - μ tanθ)]. When μ = 0, v₀ = √(rg tanθ).', 'Free-body forces (2M) + Derivation (2.5M) + μ=0 reduction (0.5M)', NULL, 'Resolve N cosθ = mg + f sinθ and N sinθ + f cosθ = mv²/r with f_max = μN. Divide equations to obtain v_max = √[rg (μ + tanθ)/(1 - μ tanθ)]. When μ = 0, v₀ = √(rg tanθ).', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000030', '(i) Acceleration of connected blocks m₁=4 kg and m₂=6 kg pulled by 20 N on smooth floor. (ii) Tension T in the light string between them. (iii) Minimum force needed if μ=0.2 (g=10 m/s²). (iv) Why is tension equal throughout a massless string?', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Physics', 'Laws of Motion', 4, '(i) a = 20/(4+6) = 2 m/s². (ii) If 20 N pulls m₂, T = m₁a = 8 N. (iii) f_max = 0.2×10×10 = 20 N. (iv) Net force on massless element (m->0) gives T₁=T₂.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Two wooden blocks of masses 4 kg and 6 kg linked by a light inextensible string rest on a horizontal table while a 20 N horizontal force pulls the 6 kg block.', '(i) a = 20/(4+6) = 2 m/s². (ii) If 20 N pulls m₂, T = m₁a = 8 N. (iii) f_max = 0.2×10×10 = 20 N. (iv) Net force on massless element (m->0) gives T₁=T₂.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000031', 'Work done by centripetal force on a satellite orbiting Earth in a circular orbit is:', '{"A":"Positive","B":"Negative","C":"Zero","D":"Infinite"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Work, Energy and Power', 1, 'Force is always perpendicular (90°) to instantaneous displacement.', NULL, NULL, 'Force is always perpendicular (90°) to instantaneous displacement.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000032', 'Two bodies of masses m and 4m move with equal kinetic energies. Ratio of their linear momenta is:', '{"A":"1 : 4","B":"1 : 2","C":"2 : 1","D":"4 : 1"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Work, Energy and Power', 1, 'p = √(2mK) => p₁/p₂ = √(1/4) = 1/2.', NULL, NULL, 'p = √(2mK) => p₁/p₂ = √(1/4) = 1/2.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000033', 'In a perfectly elastic head-on collision between two identical bodies, the bodies:', '{"A":"Stick together","B":"Exchange their velocities completely","C":"Lose 50% KE","D":"Stop both"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Work, Energy and Power', 1, 'Equal masses in 1D elastic collision swap velocities.', NULL, NULL, 'Equal masses in 1D elastic collision swap velocities.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000034', 'A spring compressed by x stores elastic potential energy ½kx². If compression is doubled to 2x, energy becomes:', '{"A":"2 times","B":"4 times","C":"8 times","D":"Half"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Work, Energy and Power', 1, 'U ∝ x² => 4 times.', NULL, NULL, 'U ∝ x² => 4 times.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000035', '1 horsepower (HP) equals:', '{"A":"500 W","B":"746 W","C":"1000 W","D":"735 W"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Work, Energy and Power', 1, '1 HP = 746 Watts.', NULL, NULL, '1 HP = 746 Watts.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000036', 'Assertion (A): Work done by a conservative force over any closed loop path is always zero.
Reason (R): Work done by conservative forces like gravity depends only on initial and final positions, not on the path followed.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Physics', 'Work, Energy and Power', 1, 'Same start and end -> ΔU = 0 => W_closed = 0.', NULL, NULL, 'Same start and end -> ΔU = 0 => W_closed = 0.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000037', 'State and prove the Work-Energy Theorem for a constant force.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Work, Energy and Power', 2, 'v² - u² = 2as => Multiply by m/2: ½mv² - ½mu² = mas = F·s = W.', 'Statement (1M) + Proof (1M)', NULL, 'v² - u² = 2as => Multiply by m/2: ½mv² - ½mu² = mas = F·s = W.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000038', 'Find angle between F = (3î + 4ĵ - 5k̂) N and displacement d = (5î + 4ĵ + 3k̂) m.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Work, Energy and Power', 2, 'F·d = 15 + 16 - 15 = 16. |F|=√50, |d|=√50 => cosθ = 16/50 = 0.32 -> θ = cos⁻¹(0.32).', 'Dot product (1M) + Angle (1M)', NULL, 'F·d = 15 + 16 - 15 = 16. |F|=√50, |d|=√50 => cosθ = 16/50 = 0.32 -> θ = cos⁻¹(0.32).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000039', 'Show that total mechanical energy (K + U) of a body falling freely under gravity remains conserved at top, mid-point h'', and ground.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Work, Energy and Power', 3, 'Top: mgh + 0 = mgh. Mid (height h-y): mg(h-y) + mgy = mgh. Bottom: 0 + mgh = mgh.', '3 heights × 1M', NULL, 'Top: mgh + 0 = mgh. Mid (height h-y): mg(h-y) + mgy = mgh. Bottom: 0 + mgh = mgh.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003a', 'Distinguish between Elastic and Inelastic collisions on momentum conservation, kinetic energy conservation, and nature of forces.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Work, Energy and Power', 3, 'Linear momentum is conserved in both (if F_ext=0); KE is conserved only in elastic (lost as heat/sound/deformation in inelastic); conservative forces act in elastic collisions.', '3 comparison points × 1M', NULL, 'Linear momentum is conserved in both (if F_ext=0); KE is conserved only in elastic (lost as heat/sound/deformation in inelastic); conservative forces act in elastic collisions.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003b', 'Derive expressions for final velocities v₁ and v₂ after a 1D perfectly elastic head-on collision of mass m₁ (initial u₁) with m₂ (initial u₂). Discuss special cases: (i) m₁ = m₂, (ii) heavy truck hitting stationary bicycle.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Physics', 'Work, Energy and Power', 5, 'Using m₁u₁+m₂u₂ = m₁v₁+m₂v₂ and relative velocity u₁-u₂ = v₂-v₁ -> v₁ = [(m₁-m₂)u₁ + 2m₂u₂]/(m₁+m₂), v₂ = [(m₂-m₁)u₂ + 2m₁u₁]/(m₁+m₂). (i) swap velocities; (ii) truck keeps ~u₁, bicycle shoots forward at ~2u₁.', 'Derivation (3.5M) + Two cases (1.5M)', NULL, 'Using m₁u₁+m₂u₂ = m₁v₁+m₂v₂ and relative velocity u₁-u₂ = v₂-v₁ -> v₁ = [(m₁-m₂)u₁ + 2m₂u₂]/(m₁+m₂), v₂ = [(m₂-m₁)u₂ + 2m₁u₁]/(m₁+m₂). (i) swap velocities; (ii) truck keeps ~u₁, bicycle shoots forward at ~2u₁.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003c', '(i) Formula for elastic potential energy of compressed spring. (ii) Find compression energy if k = 1000 N/m and x = 0.1 m. (iii) Speed of 0.5 kg block leaving horizontal smooth spring. (iv) Why is spring force conservative?', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Physics', 'Work, Energy and Power', 4, '(i) ½kx². (ii) ½(1000)(0.01) = 5 J. (iii) ½(0.5)v² = 5 => v² = 20 => v = 2√5 m/s. (iv) Work depends only on endpoints x_i and x_f.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'A horizontal bumper spring of spring constant k = 1000 N/m is compressed by 10 cm on a frictionless air track and released against a 0.5 kg glider.', '(i) ½kx². (ii) ½(1000)(0.01) = 5 J. (iii) ½(0.5)v² = 5 => v² = 20 => v = 2√5 m/s. (iv) Work depends only on endpoints x_i and x_f.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003d', 'Moment of inertia of a uniform solid sphere of mass M and radius R about its diameter is:', '{"A":"⅔ MR²","B":"⅖ MR²","C":"½ MR²","D":"MR²"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'System of Particles and Rotational Motion', 1, 'Solid sphere I = 2/5 MR²; hollow spherical shell = 2/3 MR².', NULL, NULL, 'Solid sphere I = 2/5 MR²; hollow spherical shell = 2/3 MR².', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003e', 'Relation between Torque τ and Angular Momentum L is:', '{"A":"τ = L × t","B":"τ = dL/dt","C":"L = dτ/dt","D":"τ = I + α"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'System of Particles and Rotational Motion', 1, 'Rotational analogue of F = dp/dt is τ = dL/dt.', NULL, NULL, 'Rotational analogue of F = dp/dt is τ = dL/dt.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000003f', 'When an ice skater pulls her outstretched arms close to her body, her angular speed increases due to conservation of:', '{"A":"Linear momentum","B":"Angular momentum (Iω = constant)","C":"Torque","D":"Rotational KE only"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'System of Particles and Rotational Motion', 1, 'Reducing I increases ω so L = Iω remains constant.', NULL, NULL, 'Reducing I increases ω so L = Iω remains constant.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000040', 'Centre of mass of two particles m₁ and m₂ separated by distance d lies from m₁ at distance:', '{"A":"m₁d/(m₁+m₂)","B":"m₂d/(m₁+m₂)","C":"d/2","D":"(m₁+m₂)/d"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'System of Particles and Rotational Motion', 1, 'r₁ = m₂d/(m₁+m₂), closer to heavier mass.', NULL, NULL, 'r₁ = m₂d/(m₁+m₂), closer to heavier mass.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000041', 'Moment of inertia depends on:', '{"A":"Angular velocity ω","B":"Mass and its distribution relative to the axis of rotation","C":"Torque only","D":"Force applied"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'System of Particles and Rotational Motion', 1, 'I = Σ mᵢrᵢ² depends on mass & axis geometry.', NULL, NULL, 'I = Σ mᵢrᵢ² depends on mass & axis geometry.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000042', 'Assertion (A): It is easier to open a heavy door by pushing near the outer edge farthest from the hinges.
Reason (R): Torque τ = rF sinθ is directly proportional to perpendicular lever arm r from the hinge axis.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Physics', 'System of Particles and Rotational Motion', 1, 'Larger lever arm generates more torque for same force.', NULL, NULL, 'Larger lever arm generates more torque for same force.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000043', 'Find the torque of force F = (7î + 3ĵ - 5k̂) N about the origin if position vector r = (î - ĵ + k̂) m.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'System of Particles and Rotational Motion', 2, 'τ = r × F = |î ĵ k̂; 1 -1 1; 7 3 -5| = (5-3)î - (-5-7)ĵ + (3+7)k̂ = 2î + 12ĵ + 10k̂ N·m.', 'Cross-product setup (1M) + Result (1M)', NULL, 'τ = r × F = |î ĵ k̂; 1 -1 1; 7 3 -5| = (5-3)î - (-5-7)ĵ + (3+7)k̂ = 2î + 12ĵ + 10k̂ N·m.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000044', 'Define Radius of Gyration (K) and find its value for a solid disc of radius R about its central perpendicular axis.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'System of Particles and Rotational Motion', 2, 'K = √(I/M) is the radial distance from axis where whole mass concentrated gives same I. For disc I = ½MR² => K = R/√2.', 'Definition (1M) + R/√2 (1M)', NULL, 'K = √(I/M) is the radial distance from axis where whole mass concentrated gives same I. For disc I = ½MR² => K = R/√2.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000045', 'Establish the relation L = Iω between angular momentum and angular velocity for a rigid body, and state the Law of Conservation of Angular Momentum.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'System of Particles and Rotational Motion', 3, 'Σ mᵢ vᵢ rᵢ = Σ mᵢ (rᵢ ω) rᵢ = (Σ mᵢ rᵢ²)ω = Iω. If τ_ext = 0, L = Iω = constant.', 'Derivation (2M) + Conservation law (1M)', NULL, 'Σ mᵢ vᵢ rᵢ = Σ mᵢ (rᵢ ω) rᵢ = (Σ mᵢ rᵢ²)ω = Iω. If τ_ext = 0, L = Iω = constant.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000046', 'If Earth suddenly shrinks to half its present radius without changing mass, find the new duration of the day.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'System of Particles and Rotational Motion', 3, 'I₁ω₁ = I₂ω₂ => (2/5 MR²)(2π/T₁) = (2/5 M(R/2)²)(2π/T₂) => T₂ = T₁/4 = 24/4 = 6 hours.', 'Iω conservation (1.5M) + 6 hours (1.5M)', NULL, 'I₁ω₁ = I₂ω₂ => (2/5 MR²)(2π/T₁) = (2/5 M(R/2)²)(2π/T₂) => T₂ = T₁/4 = 24/4 = 6 hours.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000047', 'State Parallel Axes Theorem and Perpendicular Axes Theorem. Using them, find Moment of Inertia of a uniform ring of mass M and radius R about: (i) its diameter and (ii) a tangent in its plane.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Physics', 'System of Particles and Rotational Motion', 5, 'Theorems stated cleanly (2M). Ring about central perpendicular axis I_z = MR². Perpendicular axes: I_x + I_y = I_z => 2I_diam = MR² => I_diam = ½MR² (1.5M). Parallel axes for tangent in plane: I_tan = I_diam + MR² = (3/2)MR² (1.5M).', 'Theorems (2M) + Ring diameter & tangent (3M)', NULL, 'Theorems stated cleanly (2M). Ring about central perpendicular axis I_z = MR². Perpendicular axes: I_x + I_y = I_z => 2I_diam = MR² => I_diam = ½MR² (1.5M). Parallel axes for tangent in plane: I_tan = I_diam + MR² = (3/2)MR² (1.5M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000048', '(i) Condition for pure rolling without slipping. (ii) Ratio of rotational KE to total KE of a rolling solid cylinder. (iii) Speed of solid sphere vs hollow sphere reaching bottom of same inclined plane first. (iv) Name quantity conserved when no net external torque acts.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Physics', 'System of Particles and Rotational Motion', 4, '(i) v_cm = Rω. (ii) K_rot/K_tot = (½Iω²)/(½mv²+½Iω²) = (1/2)/(1 + 1/2) = 1/3. (iii) Solid sphere (smaller I = 2/5 MR² accelerates faster). (iv) Angular momentum.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'On an inclined plane in the physics lab, students simultaneously release a solid sphere, solid cylinder, and ring of equal mass and radius from rest at height h.', '(i) v_cm = Rω. (ii) K_rot/K_tot = (½Iω²)/(½mv²+½Iω²) = (1/2)/(1 + 1/2) = 1/3. (iii) Solid sphere (smaller I = 2/5 MR² accelerates faster). (iv) Angular momentum.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000049', 'If radius of Earth shrinks by 2% keeping mass fixed, acceleration due to gravity g on surface:', '{"A":"Decreases by 2%","B":"Increases by 4%","C":"Decreases by 4%","D":"Increases by 2%"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Gravitation', 1, 'g ∝ 1/R² => Δg/g = -2(ΔR/R) = +4%.', NULL, NULL, 'g ∝ 1/R² => Δg/g = -2(ΔR/R) = +4%.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004a', 'Escape velocity from Earth''s surface (v_e) and orbital speed just near surface (v_o) are related by:', '{"A":"v_e = 2 v_o","B":"v_e = √2 v_o","C":"v_e = v_o/√2","D":"v_e = v_o"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Gravitation', 1, 'v_e = √(2gR) = √2 √(gR) = √2 v_o.', NULL, NULL, 'v_e = √(2gR) = √2 √(gR) = √2 v_o.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004b', 'Kepler''s second law (equal areas swept in equal times) is a direct consequence of conservation of:', '{"A":"Energy","B":"Linear momentum","C":"Angular momentum","D":"Mass"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Gravitation', 1, 'dA/dt = L/(2m) = constant because central gravitational torque is zero.', NULL, NULL, 'dA/dt = L/(2m) = constant because central gravitational torque is zero.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004c', 'Weight of a body at the centre of the Earth is:', '{"A":"Equal to surface weight","B":"Zero","C":"Infinite","D":"Half of surface weight"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Gravitation', 1, 'g_centre = 0 due to symmetric mass shell cancellation.', NULL, NULL, 'g_centre = 0 due to symmetric mass shell cancellation.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004d', 'Gravitational potential energy of mass m at distance r from Earth''s centre (mass M) is:', '{"A":"-GMm/r","B":"+GMm/r","C":"-GMm/r²","D":"-mgR"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Gravitation', 1, 'U = -GMm/r (bound negative system).', NULL, NULL, 'U = -GMm/r (bound negative system).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004e', 'Assertion (A): Astronauts inside an orbiting International Space Station experience weightlessness.
Reason (R): Both astronauts and the spacecraft are in continuous state of free fall toward Earth with identical centripetal acceleration g''.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Physics', 'Gravitation', 1, 'Normal reaction R = 0 in free-fall orbit.', NULL, NULL, 'Normal reaction R = 0 in free-fall orbit.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000004f', 'At what height above Earth''s surface does acceleration due to gravity become 25% (1/4th) of its surface value?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Gravitation', 2, 'g'' = g [R/(R+h)]² = g/4 => R/(R+h) = 1/2 => h = R ≈ 6400 km.', 'Formula (1M) + h=R (1M)', NULL, 'g'' = g [R/(R+h)]² = g/4 => R/(R+h) = 1/2 => h = R ≈ 6400 km.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000050', 'State Kepler''s Third Law of Periods and write its equation.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Gravitation', 2, 'Square of orbital period T of a planet around the Sun is proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis a of its elliptical orbit: T² ∝ a³.', 'Statement + T² ∝ a³ (2M)', NULL, 'Square of orbital period T of a planet around the Sun is proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis a of its elliptical orbit: T² ∝ a³.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000051', 'Derive how acceleration due to gravity g varies with depth d below the Earth''s surface. What happens at d = R?', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Gravitation', 3, 'Enclosed mass M'' = (4/3)π(R-d)³ρ => g'' = G M''/(R-d)² = g (1 - d/R). At d = R (centre), g'' = 0.', 'Density & enclosed mass (2M) + g(1-d/R) & g=0 (1M)', NULL, 'Enclosed mass M'' = (4/3)π(R-d)³ρ => g'' = G M''/(R-d)² = g (1 - d/R). At d = R (centre), g'' = 0.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000052', 'Calculate escape velocity from Earth (R = 6400 km, g = 9.8 m/s²) and explain why Moon has no atmosphere.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Gravitation', 3, 'v_e = √(2gR) = √(2 × 9.8 × 6.4×10⁶) ≈ 11.2 km/s. Moon''s v_e (~2.38 km/s) is lower than thermal rms speeds of gas molecules, so atmosphere escaped.', '11.2 km/s derivation/calc (2M) + Moon reason (1M)', NULL, 'v_e = √(2gR) = √(2 × 9.8 × 6.4×10⁶) ≈ 11.2 km/s. Moon''s v_e (~2.38 km/s) is lower than thermal rms speeds of gas molecules, so atmosphere escaped.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000053', 'Derive the expression for Orbital Velocity (v_o), Time Period (T), and Total Mechanical Energy (E = K + U) of an Earth satellite orbiting at height h. Explain the physical significance of negative total energy.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Physics', 'Gravitation', 5, 'GMm/(R+h)² = mv_o²/(R+h) => v_o = √(GM/(R+h)); T = 2π√((R+h)³/GM); K = +GMm/2r, U = -GMm/r -> E = -GMm/2r. Negative E signifies satellite is gravitationally bound to Earth.', 'v_o & T (2.5M) + E derivation & binding sign (2.5M)', NULL, 'GMm/(R+h)² = mv_o²/(R+h) => v_o = √(GM/(R+h)); T = 2π√((R+h)³/GM); K = +GMm/2r, U = -GMm/r -> E = -GMm/2r. Negative E signifies satellite is gravitationally bound to Earth.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000054', '(i) Height of a geostationary satellite above Earth''s equator. (ii) Time period and direction of revolution of geostationary satellite. (iii) Why are polar satellites ideal for weather forecasting and remote sensing? (iv) Escape speed ratio if planet has twice Earth''s mass and double Earth''s radius.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Physics', 'Gravitation', 4, '(i) ~35,800 km (~36,000 km). (ii) 24 hours, West to East in equatorial plane. (iii) Low altitude (~500–800 km) crossing north-south poles scans entire Earth strip-by-strip in high resolution. (iv) √(2M/2R) -> 1 : 1 (unchanged).', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Telecommunication satellites appear stationary above the equator to relay live TV signals, whereas polar earth-observation satellites pass overhead multiple times daily.', '(i) ~35,800 km (~36,000 km). (ii) 24 hours, West to East in equatorial plane. (iii) Low altitude (~500–800 km) crossing north-south poles scans entire Earth strip-by-strip in high resolution. (iv) √(2M/2R) -> 1 : 1 (unchanged).', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000055', 'Within the elastic limit, the ratio of longitudinal stress to longitudinal strain is called:', '{"A":"Bulk modulus","B":"Young''s modulus","C":"Shear modulus","D":"Poisson''s ratio"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Solids', 1, 'Y = Stress / Strain.', NULL, NULL, 'Y = Stress / Strain.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000056', 'Which material among Steel, Copper, Brass, and Rubber has the highest Young''s modulus (is most elastic)?', '{"A":"Rubber","B":"Copper","C":"Steel","D":"Brass"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Solids', 1, 'Steel resists deformation strongly (Y ≈ 2×10¹¹ N/m²), hence most elastic.', NULL, NULL, 'Steel resists deformation strongly (Y ≈ 2×10¹¹ N/m²), hence most elastic.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000057', 'SI unit and dimensions of Modulus of Elasticity are same as those of:', '{"A":"Force","B":"Pressure / Stress (N/m² or Pa)","C":"Energy","D":"Surface tension"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Solids', 1, 'Strain is dimensionless, so [Modulus] = [Stress] = [ML⁻¹T⁻²].', NULL, NULL, 'Strain is dimensionless, so [Modulus] = [Stress] = [ML⁻¹T⁻²].', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000058', 'Elastic potential energy stored per unit volume in a stretched wire is:', '{"A":"Stress × Strain","B":"½ × Stress × Strain","C":"Y × Strain","D":"Stress / Strain"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Solids', 1, 'u = ½ σ ε = ½ Y ε² = σ²/(2Y).', NULL, NULL, 'u = ½ σ ε = ½ Y ε² = σ²/(2Y).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000059', 'Poisson''s ratio is defined as:', '{"A":"Longitudinal strain / Lateral strain","B":"Lateral strain / Longitudinal strain","C":"Stress / Strain","D":"Force / Area"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Solids', 1, 'σ = (ΔD/D) / (ΔL/L).', NULL, NULL, 'σ = (ΔD/D) / (ΔL/L).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005a', 'Assertion (A): Railway tracks and heavy bridge girders are made with an I-shaped cross-section rather than solid rectangular bars.
Reason (R): I-section provides large load-bearing strength against bending sag while saving weight and steel cost.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Solids', 1, 'Flanges carry maximum compressive/tensile bending stress.', NULL, NULL, 'Flanges carry maximum compressive/tensile bending stress.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005b', 'Define Elastomers with one example and state how their stress-strain curve differs from metals.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Solids', 2, 'Materials that can be stretched to very large strains without permanent deformation (e.g., vulcanised rubber, aorta tissue). Their stress-strain graph is non-linear with virtually no plastic region and doesn''t obey Hooke''s law over large extensions.', 'Definition/example (1M) + Curve feature (1M)', NULL, 'Materials that can be stretched to very large strains without permanent deformation (e.g., vulcanised rubber, aorta tissue). Their stress-strain graph is non-linear with virtually no plastic region and doesn''t obey Hooke''s law over large extensions.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005c', 'A steel wire of length 2 m and cross-section 1 mm² stretches by 0.5 mm under a load. Find longitudinal strain and stress if Y = 2×10¹¹ N/m².', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Solids', 2, 'Strain = 0.5×10⁻³ / 2 = 2.5×10⁻⁴. Stress = Y × Strain = 2×10¹¹ × 2.5×10⁻⁴ = 5×10⁷ N/m².', 'Strain (1M) + Stress (1M)', NULL, 'Strain = 0.5×10⁻³ / 2 = 2.5×10⁻⁴. Stress = Y × Strain = 2×10¹¹ × 2.5×10⁻⁴ = 5×10⁷ N/m².', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005d', 'Sketch/describe the complete Stress–Strain curve for a metal wire loaded up to fracture, clearly explaining Proportional limit, Yield point (Elastic limit), Permanent set, and Ultimate tensile strength.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Solids', 3, 'Proportional limit (Hooke linear), Elastic limit/Yield point (returns upon unloading), Plastic deformation + Permanent set (wire keeps elongation after load removal), UTS (peak stress), Fracture point (neck breaks).', '4–5 key landmarks cleanly explained (3M)', NULL, 'Proportional limit (Hooke linear), Elastic limit/Yield point (returns upon unloading), Plastic deformation + Permanent set (wire keeps elongation after load removal), UTS (peak stress), Fracture point (neck breaks).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005e', 'Why are steel ropes used in heavy cranes? Calculate minimum cross-sectional area of a steel cable (elastic limit = 3×10⁸ N/m²) needed to lift 10 metric tons (g = 10 m/s²).', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Solids', 3, 'Steel has high elastic limit & Young''s modulus. F = 10⁴ kg × 10 = 10⁵ N. A ≥ F/σ_max = 10⁵ / (3×10⁸) = 3.33 × 10⁻⁴ m² (≈ 3.33 cm²).', 'Reason (1M) + Area calculation (2M)', NULL, 'Steel has high elastic limit & Young''s modulus. F = 10⁴ kg × 10 = 10⁵ N. A ≥ F/σ_max = 10⁵ / (3×10⁸) = 3.33 × 10⁻⁴ m² (≈ 3.33 cm²).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000005f', 'Derive the expression for Elastic Potential Energy stored per unit volume of a stretched wire (u = ½ stress × strain). Also explain why bridges become unsafe after prolonged repeated heavy traffic ("elastic fatigue") and why maximum mountain height on Earth is limited to ~10 km.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Solids', 5, 'Derivation dW = F dl = (YA l/L) dl -> W = ½ (YA/L)ΔL² -> u = W/(AL) = ½ σ ε (3M). Repeated alternating stress weakens crystal slip planes causing elastic fatigue (1M). Base rock shear strength (~3×10⁸ Pa) supports ρgh ≤ shear limit => h_max ≈ 10 km (1M).', 'Derivation (3M) + Elastic fatigue (1M) + Mountain height (1M)', NULL, 'Derivation dW = F dl = (YA l/L) dl -> W = ½ (YA/L)ΔL² -> u = W/(AL) = ½ σ ε (3M). Repeated alternating stress weakens crystal slip planes causing elastic fatigue (1M). Base rock shear strength (~3×10⁸ Pa) supports ρgh ≤ shear limit => h_max ≈ 10 km (1M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000060', '(i) Write expression for depression δ of a rectangular beam of length l, breadth b, depth d under central load W. (ii) To minimize sag, should depth d or breadth b be made larger? (iii) Explain buckling vs bending. (iv) Name one biological tissue acting as an elastomer.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Solids', 4, '(i) δ = Wl³ / (4Ybd³). (ii) Increase depth d (since δ ∝ 1/d³). (iii) Sideways twisting under compression is buckling, downward curve under load is bending. (iv) Aorta arterial wall.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Civil engineers designing a highway flyover test rectangular steel girders to minimise mid-span downward sag δ under peak truck traffic.', '(i) δ = Wl³ / (4Ybd³). (ii) Increase depth d (since δ ∝ 1/d³). (iii) Sideways twisting under compression is buckling, downward curve under load is bending. (iv) Aorta arterial wall.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000061', 'Hydraulic brakes and hydraulic lifts operate directly on:', '{"A":"Archimedes Principle","B":"Pascal''s Law","C":"Bernoulli''s Theorem","D":"Torricelli''s Law"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Fluids', 1, 'Pressure applied to an enclosed incompressible fluid transmits undiminished.', NULL, NULL, 'Pressure applied to an enclosed incompressible fluid transmits undiminished.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000062', 'Dynamic lift of an aeroplane wing (aerofoil) is explained by:', '{"A":"Stoke''s Law","B":"Bernoulli''s Principle","C":"Surface Tension","D":"Poiseuille''s Formula"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Fluids', 1, 'Higher airspeed over curved top creates lower pressure than underneath.', NULL, NULL, 'Higher airspeed over curved top creates lower pressure than underneath.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000063', 'Raindrops acquire spherical shape due to:', '{"A":"Viscosity","B":"Surface tension minimizing surface area for a given volume","C":"Gravity","D":"Air drag"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Fluids', 1, 'Sphere has minimum surface area for fixed volume.', NULL, NULL, 'Sphere has minimum surface area for fixed volume.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000064', 'Terminal velocity v_t of a small spherical ball of radius r falling through a viscous liquid is proportional to:', '{"A":"r","B":"r²","C":"1/r","D":"√r"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Fluids', 1, 'Stoke''s law: 6πηrv_t = (4/3)πr³(ρ-σ)g => v_t ∝ r².', NULL, NULL, 'Stoke''s law: 6πηrv_t = (4/3)πr³(ρ-σ)g => v_t ∝ r².', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000065', 'Addition of soap/detergent to water helps clean oily clothes by:', '{"A":"Increasing surface tension","B":"Lowering surface tension of water so it wets fabric pores easily","C":"Increasing viscosity","D":"Raising boiling point"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Fluids', 1, 'Detergent reduces surface tension and contact angle.', NULL, NULL, 'Detergent reduces surface tension and contact angle.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000066', 'Assertion (A): A deep water dam is constructed much thicker at the bottom than at the top.
Reason (R): Hydrostatic pressure P = hρg increases linearly with depth h of water.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Fluids', 1, 'Huge horizontal water thrust acts near the dam base.', NULL, NULL, 'Huge horizontal water thrust acts near the dam base.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000067', 'State Equation of Continuity for steady incompressible fluid flow. Why does water speed up when you pinch a garden hose nozzle?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Fluids', 2, 'A₁v₁ = A₂v₂ = constant. Reducing cross-sectional area A₂ forces flow speed v₂ to increase proportionally to conserve mass flux.', 'Formula (1M) + Reason (1M)', NULL, 'A₁v₁ = A₂v₂ = constant. Reducing cross-sectional area A₂ forces flow speed v₂ to increase proportionally to conserve mass flux.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000068', 'Why does hot soup taste better than cold soup in terms of surface tension?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Fluids', 2, 'Warm liquids have lower surface tension, allowing hot soup to spread smoothly over a larger area of taste buds on the tongue.', 'Lower T -> wider spreading (2M)', NULL, 'Warm liquids have lower surface tension, allowing hot soup to spread smoothly over a larger area of taste buds on the tongue.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000069', 'Derive Excess Pressure inside a liquid drop of radius r and surface tension T (ΔP = 2T/r), and inside a soap bubble (4T/r).', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Fluids', 3, 'Virtual work ΔP(4πr² dr) = T(8πr dr) => ΔP = 2T/r. Soap bubble has two free surfaces (inner + outer), doubling to 4T/r.', 'Drop derivation (2M) + Soap bubble factor 2 (1M)', NULL, 'Virtual work ΔP(4πr² dr) = T(8πr dr) => ΔP = 2T/r. Soap bubble has two free surfaces (inner + outer), doubling to 4T/r.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006a', 'State Torricelli''s Law and derive the speed of efflux of liquid emerging from an orifice at depth h below free surface.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Fluids', 3, 'Using Bernoulli between top surface (v₁≈0, P=P_a, y₁=h) and orifice (P=P_a, y₂=0): ½ρv² = ρgh => v = √(2gh) (same as free fall).', 'Bernoulli setup (2M) + v = √(2gh) (1M)', NULL, 'Using Bernoulli between top surface (v₁≈0, P=P_a, y₁=h) and orifice (P=P_a, y₂=0): ½ρv² = ρgh => v = √(2gh) (same as free fall).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006b', 'State Bernoulli''s Principle and derive P + ½ρv² + ρgh = constant for streamline flow of an ideal incompressible non-viscous fluid. State two real-life applications.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Fluids', 5, 'Equate net pressure work (P₁-P₂)ΔV = ΔK + ΔU -> P₁ + ½ρv₁² + ρgh₁ = P₂ + ½ρv₂² + ρgh₂ (3.5M). Applications: Venturimeter, aerofoil lift, atomiser/sprayer, spinning Magnus ball (1.5M).', 'Statement & derivation (3.5M) + Applications (1.5M)', NULL, 'Equate net pressure work (P₁-P₂)ΔV = ΔK + ΔU -> P₁ + ½ρv₁² + ρgh₁ = P₂ + ½ρv₂² + ρgh₂ (3.5M). Applications: Venturimeter, aerofoil lift, atomiser/sprayer, spinning Magnus ball (1.5M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006c', '(i) Write Ascent Formula for capillary rise h. (ii) If tube radius is halved, what happens to rise height h? (iii) Will water rise or depress in a silver/glass capillary with θ < 90° vs mercury (θ > 90°)? (iv) Give one everyday application of capillarity.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Fluids', 4, '(i) h = 2T cosθ / (rρg). (ii) Doubles (h ∝ 1/r). (iii) Rises for θ < 90° (water/glass); depresses for θ > 90° (mercury/glass). (iv) Oil rising in lamp wick, blotting paper, water rising in plant xylem/soil pores.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'A clean glass capillary tube of radius 0.5 mm dipped vertically into a beaker of pure water shows water climbing rapidly up the narrow bore into a concave meniscus.', '(i) h = 2T cosθ / (rρg). (ii) Doubles (h ∝ 1/r). (iii) Rises for θ < 90° (water/glass); depresses for θ > 90° (mercury/glass). (iv) Oil rising in lamp wick, blotting paper, water rising in plant xylem/soil pores.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006d', 'Temperature at which Celsius and Fahrenheit scales read the exact same numerical value is:', '{"A":"-273°","B":"-40°","C":"0°","D":"32°"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Thermal Properties of Matter', 1, 'C = (5/9)(C - 32) => C = -40°.', NULL, NULL, 'C = (5/9)(C - 32) => C = -40°.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006e', 'Water exhibits maximum density at temperature:', '{"A":"0°C","B":"4°C","C":"10°C","D":"100°C"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Thermal Properties of Matter', 1, 'Anomalous expansion makes water densest at 4°C (277 K), saving aquatic life beneath frozen lakes.', NULL, NULL, 'Anomalous expansion makes water densest at 4°C (277 K), saving aquatic life beneath frozen lakes.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000006f', 'Relation among linear (α), superficial (β), and cubical (γ) thermal expansion coefficients of an isotropic solid is:', '{"A":"α : β : γ = 1 : 2 : 3","B":"1 : 3 : 9","C":"3 : 2 : 1","D":"1 : 1 : 1"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Thermal Properties of Matter', 1, 'β = 2α and γ = 3α.', NULL, NULL, 'β = 2α and γ = 3α.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000070', 'Steam at 100°C causes far more severe burns than boiling water at 100°C because steam carries extra:', '{"A":"Specific heat","B":"Latent heat of vaporisation (≈ 2260 J/g)","C":"Thermal conductivity","D":"Pressure"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Thermal Properties of Matter', 1, 'Condensation releases huge latent heat on skin.', NULL, NULL, 'Condensation releases huge latent heat on skin.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000071', 'Wien''s Displacement Law relates peak radiation wavelength λ_m and absolute temperature T as:', '{"A":"λ_m / T = constant","B":"λ_m × T = constant (b)","C":"λ_m × T² = constant","D":"λ_m ∝ T⁴"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Thermal Properties of Matter', 1, 'Hotter stars peak at shorter blue wavelengths (λ_m T = b).', NULL, NULL, 'Hotter stars peak at shorter blue wavelengths (λ_m T = b).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000072', 'Assertion (A): Black clothes are preferred in winter while white clothes feel cooler in summer.
Reason (R): Black surfaces are good absorbers and emitters of thermal radiation, whereas white surfaces reflect most incident radiation.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Physics', 'Thermal Properties of Matter', 1, 'Absorptivity = emissivity (Kirchhoff''s law).', NULL, NULL, 'Absorptivity = emissivity (Kirchhoff''s law).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000073', 'Define Coefficient of Linear Expansion (α) and state why small gaps are left between iron railway rails.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Thermal Properties of Matter', 2, 'α = ΔL / (L₀ ΔT). Gaps accommodate summer thermal expansion so rails do not buckle sideways.', '1M each', NULL, 'α = ΔL / (L₀ ΔT). Gaps accommodate summer thermal expansion so rails do not buckle sideways.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000074', 'State Newton''s Law of Cooling and draw the qualitative temperature-vs-time cooling curve.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Thermal Properties of Matter', 2, '-dQ/dt = k(T - T₀) — rate of cooling is proportional to excess temperature above surroundings (valid for small ΔT). Curve decays exponentially toward ambient T₀.', 'Law (1M) + Curve/assumption (1M)', NULL, '-dQ/dt = k(T - T₀) — rate of cooling is proportional to excess temperature above surroundings (valid for small ΔT). Curve decays exponentially toward ambient T₀.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000075', 'A pan filled with hot food cools from 94°C to 86°C in 2 minutes when room temperature is 20°C. How long will it take to cool from 71°C to 69°C?', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Thermal Properties of Matter', 3, 'Average T₁ = 90°C -> (94-86)/2 = K(90-20) => K = 4/70. Average T₂ = 70°C -> (71-69)/t = (4/70)(70-20) = 200/70 => t = 2×70/200 = 0.7 min = 42 seconds.', 'First K eval (1.5M) + 42 s (1.5M)', NULL, 'Average T₁ = 90°C -> (94-86)/2 = K(90-20) => K = 4/70. Average T₂ = 70°C -> (71-69)/t = (4/70)(70-20) = 200/70 => t = 2×70/200 = 0.7 min = 42 seconds.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000076', 'Compare Conduction, Convection, and Radiation modes of heat transfer on medium requirement and speed.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Thermal Properties of Matter', 3, 'Conduction: solid medium, lattice vibration/free e⁻, slow. Convection: fluid bulk motion (gravity-driven), moderate. Radiation: no medium required (EM waves), travels at speed of light.', '3 × 1M', NULL, 'Conduction: solid medium, lattice vibration/free e⁻, slow. Convection: fluid bulk motion (gravity-driven), moderate. Radiation: no medium required (EM waves), travels at speed of light.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000077', 'Define Latent Heat of Fusion and Vaporisation. Calculate final state and temperature when 2 kg of ice at -10°C is mixed with 0.5 kg of steam at 100°C in a calorimeter (c_ice=2100 J/kgK, L_f=3.35×10⁵ J/kg, c_w=4186 J/kgK, L_v=2.25×10⁶ J/kg).', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Physics', 'Thermal Properties of Matter', 5, 'Heat needed to melt all ice to 0°C water = 2×2100×10 + 2×3.35×10⁵ = 42000 + 670000 = 712,000 J. Heat released by 0.5 kg steam condensing at 100°C = 0.5×2.25×10⁶ = 1,125,000 J (>712 kJ). Equating heat to warm 2.5 kg mixture: let final T < 100°C -> 712,000 + 2×4186×T = 1,125,000 + 0.5×4186×(100-T) -> 10465 T = 622,300 => T ≈ 59.5°C water.', 'Definitions (1.5M) + Calorimetry calculation ~59.5°C (3.5M)', NULL, 'Heat needed to melt all ice to 0°C water = 2×2100×10 + 2×3.35×10⁵ = 42000 + 670000 = 712,000 J. Heat released by 0.5 kg steam condensing at 100°C = 0.5×2.25×10⁶ = 1,125,000 J (>712 kJ). Equating heat to warm 2.5 kg mixture: let final T < 100°C -> 712,000 + 2×4186×T = 1,125,000 + 0.5×4186×(100-T) -> 10465 T = 622,300 => T ≈ 59.5°C water.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000078', '(i) Why do birds puff up feathers in cold winter? (ii) State Stefan-Boltzmann Law for blackbody radiation. (iii) Why are double-pane glass windows energy efficient? (iv) What is the thermal conductivity order of Silver, Wood, Air?', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Physics', 'Thermal Properties of Matter', 4, '(i) Trapped air pockets act as poor conductors preventing body heat loss. (ii) E = σT⁴ per unit area per second. (iii) Sealed air gap blocks conduction and convection. (iv) Silver > Wood > Air.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Architects in Ladakh incorporate triple-glazed walls, woollen textiles, and dark passive solar Trombe walls to retain daytime solar warmth through freezing nights.', '(i) Trapped air pockets act as poor conductors preventing body heat loss. (ii) E = σT⁴ per unit area per second. (iii) Sealed air gap blocks conduction and convection. (iv) Silver > Wood > Air.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000079', 'Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics leads directly to the concept of:', '{"A":"Internal energy","B":"Temperature (thermal equilibrium)","C":"Entropy","D":"Work"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Thermodynamics', 1, 'Two systems in thermal equilibrium with a third share equal temperature.', NULL, NULL, 'Two systems in thermal equilibrium with a third share equal temperature.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007a', 'First Law of Thermodynamics ΔQ = ΔU + ΔW is a statement of conservation of:', '{"A":"Momentum","B":"Energy","C":"Mass","D":"Charge"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Thermodynamics', 1, 'Heat supplied equals internal energy rise plus mechanical work done.', NULL, NULL, 'Heat supplied equals internal energy rise plus mechanical work done.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007b', 'In an isothermal expansion of an ideal gas at constant temperature T:', '{"A":"ΔU = 0 so ΔQ = ΔW","B":"ΔQ = 0","C":"ΔW = 0","D":"Pressure remains constant"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Thermodynamics', 1, 'U of ideal gas depends only on T -> ΔT=0 => ΔU=0.', NULL, NULL, 'U of ideal gas depends only on T -> ΔT=0 => ΔU=0.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007c', 'Slope of an adiabatic curve on a P–V diagram compared to an isothermal curve is:', '{"A":"Flatter","B":"Steeper by factor γ (= C_p/C_v)","C":"Equal","D":"Zero"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Thermodynamics', 1, 'Adiabatic slope = -γ(P/V) vs Isothermal -P/V.', NULL, NULL, 'Adiabatic slope = -γ(P/V) vs Isothermal -P/V.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007d', 'Mayer''s relation between molar specific heats C_p and C_v of an ideal gas is:', '{"A":"C_p + C_v = R","B":"C_p - C_v = R","C":"C_p / C_v = R","D":"C_v - C_p = R"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Thermodynamics', 1, 'C_p - C_v = R (in J/mol K).', NULL, NULL, 'C_p - C_v = R (in J/mol K).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007e', 'Assertion (A): An adiabatic expansion of an ideal gas always causes its temperature to drop (cooling).
Reason (R): Since ΔQ = 0, positive expansion work ΔW = -ΔU uses up the gas''s own internal kinetic energy.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Physics', 'Thermodynamics', 1, 'Bursting tyre air cools adiabatically.', NULL, NULL, 'Bursting tyre air cools adiabatically.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000007f', 'Why does C_p exceed C_v for an ideal gas? Derive Mayer''s relation C_p - C_v = R briefly.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Thermodynamics', 2, 'At constant pressure, supplied heat must both raise temperature (ΔU = C_v ΔT) AND perform external expansion work PΔV = RΔT for 1 mole -> C_p ΔT = C_v ΔT + R ΔT => C_p - C_v = R.', 'Reason (1M) + Derivation (1M)', NULL, 'At constant pressure, supplied heat must both raise temperature (ΔU = C_v ΔT) AND perform external expansion work PΔV = RΔT for 1 mole -> C_p ΔT = C_v ΔT + R ΔT => C_p - C_v = R.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000080', 'Distinguish between Intensive and Extensive thermodynamic state variables with one example each.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Thermodynamics', 2, 'Intensive: independent of system size/mass (Temperature, Pressure, Density). Extensive: proportional to system mass/size (Volume, Internal Energy, Mass).', '1M each', NULL, 'Intensive: independent of system size/mass (Temperature, Pressure, Density). Extensive: proportional to system mass/size (Volume, Internal Energy, Mass).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000081', 'Derive the expression for Work Done during an Isothermal expansion of n moles of an ideal gas from V₁ to V₂ at temperature T.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Thermodynamics', 3, 'W = ∫ P dV = ∫ (nRT/V) dV = nRT ln(V₂/V₁) = 2.303 nRT log₁₀(V₂/V₁).', 'Integral setup (1.5M) + Formula (1.5M)', NULL, 'W = ∫ P dV = ∫ (nRT/V) dV = nRT ln(V₂/V₁) = 2.303 nRT log₁₀(V₂/V₁).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000082', 'State both Kelvin-Planck and Clausius statements of the Second Law of Thermodynamics.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Thermodynamics', 3, 'Kelvin-Planck: No cyclic process can convert 100% of absorbed heat into mechanical work without rejecting part to a sink. Clausius: Heat cannot flow spontaneously from a colder body to a hotter body without external work.', '1.5M each', NULL, 'Kelvin-Planck: No cyclic process can convert 100% of absorbed heat into mechanical work without rejecting part to a sink. Clausius: Heat cannot flow spontaneously from a colder body to a hotter body without external work.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000083', 'Derive the expression for Work Done in an Adiabatic process (P₁V₁^γ = P₂V₂^γ = K): W = nR(T₁ - T₂)/(γ - 1). Calculate work done when 2 moles of a monoatomic gas (γ = 5/3) expand adiabatically from 400 K to 300 K (R = 8.3 J/mol K).', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Physics', 'Thermodynamics', 5, 'Derivation ∫ K V^(-γ) dV -> (P₁V₁ - P₂V₂)/(γ-1) = nR(T₁-T₂)/(γ-1) (3.5M). Calculation: W = 2 × 8.3 × (400-300) / (5/3 - 1) = 1660 × 3/2 = 2490 J (1.5M).', 'Derivation (3.5M) + 2490 J calculation (1.5M)', NULL, 'Derivation ∫ K V^(-γ) dV -> (P₁V₁ - P₂V₂)/(γ-1) = nR(T₁-T₂)/(γ-1) (3.5M). Calculation: W = 2 × 8.3 × (400-300) / (5/3 - 1) = 1660 × 3/2 = 2490 J (1.5M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000084', '(i) Write work done in a cyclic process represented by a closed P-V triangle. (ii) If clockwise loop encloses base ΔV = 2 m³ and height ΔP = 300 Pa, find net work done. (iii) What is ΔU over one complete cycle? (iv) Find net heat absorbed ΔQ in this cycle.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Physics', 'Thermodynamics', 4, '(i) Enclosed area of the P-V loop. (ii) Area = ½ × 2 × 300 = +300 J. (iii) ΔU = 0 (state function returns to start). (iv) ΔQ = ΔU + ΔW = +300 J.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'An ideal gas is carried clockwise around a triangular P-V cycle A -> B -> C -> A where volume changes between 1 m³ and 3 m³ and pressure ranges between 200 Pa and 500 Pa.', '(i) Enclosed area of the P-V loop. (ii) Area = ½ × 2 × 300 = +300 J. (iii) ΔU = 0 (state function returns to start). (iv) ΔQ = ΔU + ΔW = +300 J.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000085', 'Mean translational kinetic energy per molecule of an ideal gas at absolute temperature T is:', '{"A":"½ k_B T","B":"3/2 k_B T","C":"5/2 k_B T","D":"3 k_B T"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Kinetic Theory', 1, '3 translational degrees of freedom -> 3 × (½ k_B T).', NULL, NULL, '3 translational degrees of freedom -> 3 × (½ k_B T).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000086', 'Root mean square (v_rms) speed of gas molecules is related to molar mass M as:', '{"A":"v_rms = √(3RT/M)","B":"√(2RT/M)","C":"√(8RT/πM)","D":"3RT/M"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Kinetic Theory', 1, 'v_rms = √(3RT/M) = √(3P/ρ).', NULL, NULL, 'v_rms = √(3RT/M) = √(3P/ρ).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000087', 'Ratio of specific heats γ = C_p/C_v for a rigid diatomic gas molecule (5 degrees of freedom) is:', '{"A":"5/3","B":"7/5","C":"9/7","D":"4/3"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Kinetic Theory', 1, 'f = 5 => C_v = 5R/2, C_p = 7R/2 -> γ = 7/5 = 1.4.', NULL, NULL, 'f = 5 => C_v = 5R/2, C_p = 7R/2 -> γ = 7/5 = 1.4.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000088', 'Mean free path λ of gas molecules of diameter d and number density n is:', '{"A":"1 / (√2 π n d²)","B":"√2 π n d²","C":"1 / (n d)","D":"n d²"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Kinetic Theory', 1, 'λ = 1 / (√2 π n d²).', NULL, NULL, 'λ = 1 / (√2 π n d²).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000089', 'At what temperature will rms speed of oxygen molecules be double that at 27°C (300 K)?', '{"A":"600 K","B":"900 K","C":"1200 K","D":"1500 K"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Kinetic Theory', 1, 'v_rms ∝ √T => doubling speed requires 4T = 4×300 = 1200 K (927°C).', NULL, NULL, 'v_rms ∝ √T => doubling speed requires 4T = 4×300 = 1200 K (927°C).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008a', 'Assertion (A): Real gases closely follow the ideal gas law PV = nRT at high temperature and low pressure.
Reason (R): At high temperature and low pressure, intermolecular separation is large, making molecular volume and attractive forces negligible.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Physics', 'Kinetic Theory', 1, 'KTG assumptions hold strictly in low-density limit.', NULL, NULL, 'KTG assumptions hold strictly in low-density limit.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008b', 'List any four fundamental assumptions of Kinetic Theory of Gases.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Kinetic Theory', 2, '(1) Point molecules of negligible volume compared to container. (2) Random perpetual motion colliding elastically with walls/each other. (3) Zero intermolecular attraction except during impact. (4) Collision time negligible compared to free flight time.', '0.5M × 4 = 2M', NULL, '(1) Point molecules of negligible volume compared to container. (2) Random perpetual motion colliding elastically with walls/each other. (3) Zero intermolecular attraction except during impact. (4) Collision time negligible compared to free flight time.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008c', 'State Law of Equipartition of Energy and find internal energy of 1 mole of polyatomic non-linear gas (f = 6) at temperature T.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Kinetic Theory', 2, 'Each quadratic degree of freedom contributes ½ k_B T average energy per molecule. For f=6: U = 6 × (½ RT) = 3 RT.', 'Law (1M) + U=3RT (1M)', NULL, 'Each quadratic degree of freedom contributes ½ k_B T average energy per molecule. For f=6: U = 6 × (½ RT) = 3 RT.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008d', 'Derive the pressure exerted by an ideal gas on container walls: P = ⅓ ρ v_rms².', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Kinetic Theory', 3, 'Momentum change per bounce 2mv_x; collision frequency v_x/(2L) -> Force/wall = Σ mv_x²/L -> Pressure P = (mN/V) v_x_rms² = ⅓ ρ v_rms² (by isotropy v_rms² = 3 v_x²).', 'Derivation steps cleanly (3M)', NULL, 'Momentum change per bounce 2mv_x; collision frequency v_x/(2L) -> Force/wall = Σ mv_x²/L -> Pressure P = (mN/V) v_x_rms² = ⅓ ρ v_rms² (by isotropy v_rms² = 3 v_x²).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008e', 'Calculate total random kinetic energy of 1 mole of nitrogen gas at 27°C (300 K), taking R = 8.31 J/mol K. How much is translational only?', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Kinetic Theory', 3, 'N₂ has f = 5 (3 trans + 2 rot). Total KE = (5/2)RT = 2.5 × 8.31 × 300 = 6232.5 J. Translational KE = (3/2)RT = 3739.5 J.', 'Total 6232.5 J (1.5M) + Trans 3739.5 J (1.5M)', NULL, 'N₂ has f = 5 (3 trans + 2 rot). Total KE = (5/2)RT = 2.5 × 8.31 × 300 = 6232.5 J. Translational KE = (3/2)RT = 3739.5 J.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000008f', 'Using Law of Equipartition of Energy, determine C_v, C_p, and γ = C_p/C_v for: (a) Monoatomic gas (He/Ar), (b) Rigid Diatomic gas (O₂/N₂), and (c) Non-rigid Diatomic gas with 1 vibrational mode (f=7).', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Physics', 'Kinetic Theory', 5, '(a) f=3 -> C_v=3R/2, C_p=5R/2, γ=5/3=1.67. (b) f=5 -> C_v=5R/2, C_p=7R/2, γ=7/5=1.40. (c) f=5+2=7 -> C_v=7R/2, C_p=9R/2, γ=9/7=1.29.', 'Monoatomic (1.5M) + Rigid Diatomic (1.5M) + Vibrational Diatomic (2M)', NULL, '(a) f=3 -> C_v=3R/2, C_p=5R/2, γ=5/3=1.67. (b) f=5 -> C_v=5R/2, C_p=7R/2, γ=7/5=1.40. (c) f=5+2=7 -> C_v=7R/2, C_p=9R/2, γ=9/7=1.29.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000090', '(i) Define Mean Free Path. (ii) If gas pressure doubles at constant temperature, what happens to mean free path λ? (iii) Arrange H₂, O₂, and CO₂ in decreasing order of v_rms at 300 K. (iv) State Dalton''s Law of Partial Pressures.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Physics', 'Kinetic Theory', 4, '(i) Average distance travelled by a molecule between two successive collisions. (ii) n = P/(k_B T) doubles -> λ halves. (iii) H₂ > O₂ > CO₂ (v_rms ∝ 1/√M). (iv) Total pressure of non-reacting gas mixture equals sum of partial pressures P = P₁ + P₂ + ...', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'Inside an evacuated laboratory chamber filled with trace gas mixtures at 300 K, lighter gas molecules collide far more rapidly while total pressure adds up across components.', '(i) Average distance travelled by a molecule between two successive collisions. (ii) n = P/(k_B T) doubles -> λ halves. (iii) H₂ > O₂ > CO₂ (v_rms ∝ 1/√M). (iv) Total pressure of non-reacting gas mixture equals sum of partial pressures P = P₁ + P₂ + ...', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000091', 'In Simple Harmonic Motion (SHM), restoring force is always:', '{"A":"Constant","B":"Directly proportional to displacement and directed toward mean position (F = -kx)","C":"Proportional to x²","D":"Independent of position"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Oscillations', 1, 'Defining condition: a = -ω²x.', NULL, NULL, 'Defining condition: a = -ω²x.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000092', 'Phase difference between displacement and acceleration of a particle executing SHM is:', '{"A":"0","B":"π/2","C":"π (180°)","D":"3π/2"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Oscillations', 1, 'a = -ω²x is opposite in sign (π phase) to x.', NULL, NULL, 'a = -ω²x is opposite in sign (π phase) to x.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000093', 'Time period of a simple pendulum of length L on a freely falling satellite (g_eff = 0) is:', '{"A":"Zero","B":"2π√(L/g)","C":"Infinite (does not oscillate)","D":"1 second"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Oscillations', 1, 'No restoring torque when g_eff = 0 -> T = ∞.', NULL, NULL, 'No restoring torque when g_eff = 0 -> T = ∞.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000094', 'Total energy of a particle executing SHM of amplitude A is proportional to:', '{"A":"A","B":"A²","C":"1/A","D":"√A"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Oscillations', 1, 'E = ½ m ω² A² ∝ A².', NULL, NULL, 'E = ½ m ω² A² ∝ A².', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000095', 'At what displacement from mean position in SHM are Kinetic Energy and Potential Energy equal?', '{"A":"A/2","B":"A/√2","C":"A/4","D":"0"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Oscillations', 1, '½k(A²-x²) = ½kx² => 2x² = A² => x = A/√2.', NULL, NULL, '½k(A²-x²) = ½kx² => 2x² = A² => x = A/√2.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000096', 'Assertion (A): Every oscillatory motion is necessarily periodic, whereas every periodic motion is not oscillatory.
Reason (R): Orbital revolution of Earth around Sun repeats periodically after 1 year without moving to-and-fro about a mean equilibrium point.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Physics', 'Oscillations', 1, 'Oscillation requires restoring force about equilibrium.', NULL, NULL, 'Oscillation requires restoring force about equilibrium.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000097', 'A spring of force constant k is cut into two equal halves. What is the force constant of each half and new time period of mass m on one half?', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Oscillations', 2, 'Since k ∝ 1/L, halving length doubles stiffness: k'' = 2k. T'' = 2π√(m/2k) = T/√2.', 'k''=2k (1M) + T''=T/√2 (1M)', NULL, 'Since k ∝ 1/L, halving length doubles stiffness: k'' = 2k. T'' = 2π√(m/2k) = T/√2.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000098', 'Write the differential equation of SHM and find frequency of x(t) = 0.05 cos(10π t + π/4).', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Oscillations', 2, 'd²x/dt² + ω²x = 0. Here ω = 10π = 2πf => f = 5 Hz (T = 0.2 s).', 'Diff eq (1M) + f=5 Hz (1M)', NULL, 'd²x/dt² + ω²x = 0. Here ω = 10π = 2πf => f = 5 Hz (T = 0.2 s).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-000000000099', 'Derive expressions for Kinetic Energy, Potential Energy, and Total Energy of a particle in SHM, and show Total Energy remains constant.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Oscillations', 3, 'x = A sin(ωt), v = Aω cos(ωt). U = ½k A² sin²(ωt), K = ½m ω² A² cos²(ωt). Adding K + U = ½ m ω² A² (sin² + cos²) = ½ k A² = constant.', 'K & U (2M) + Total constant (1M)', NULL, 'x = A sin(ωt), v = Aω cos(ωt). U = ½k A² sin²(ωt), K = ½m ω² A² cos²(ωt). Adding K + U = ½ m ω² A² (sin² + cos²) = ½ k A² = constant.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000009a', 'Calculate the length of a "Seconds Pendulum" on Earth (g = 9.8 m/s²). What happens if it is taken to the Moon (g_m = g/6)?', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Oscillations', 3, 'Seconds pendulum has T = 2 s -> 2 = 2π√(L/9.8) => L = 9.8/π² ≈ 0.993 m (~1 m). On Moon, T = 2√6 ≈ 4.9 s.', 'Length ~0.993 m (2M) + Moon T ~4.9 s (1M)', NULL, 'Seconds pendulum has T = 2 s -> 2 = 2π√(L/9.8) => L = 9.8/π² ≈ 0.993 m (~1 m). On Moon, T = 2√6 ≈ 4.9 s.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000009b', 'Show that small oscillations of a Simple Pendulum are Simple Harmonic, and derive its time period T = 2π√(L/g). Also find the effective time period when mass M oscillates between two springs connected (i) in series and (ii) in parallel.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Physics', 'Oscillations', 5, 'Restoring torque τ = -mgL sinθ ≈ -mgL θ -> α = -(g/L)θ -> ω = √(g/L) => T = 2π√(L/g) (3M). Series: 1/k_s = 1/k₁+1/k₂ -> T = 2π√(M(k₁+k₂)/(k₁k₂)); Parallel: k_p = k₁+k₂ -> T = 2π√(M/(k₁+k₂)) (2M).', 'Pendulum SHM derivation (3M) + Series/Parallel springs (2M)', NULL, 'Restoring torque τ = -mgL sinθ ≈ -mgL θ -> α = -(g/L)θ -> ω = √(g/L) => T = 2π√(L/g) (3M). Series: 1/k_s = 1/k₁+1/k₂ -> T = 2π√(M(k₁+k₂)/(k₁k₂)); Parallel: k_p = k₁+k₂ -> T = 2π√(M/(k₁+k₂)) (2M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000009c', '(i) Amplitude and angular frequency of y = 5 sin(4π t + π/3) cm. (ii) Maximum speed v_max. (iii) Maximum acceleration a_max. (iv) Initial displacement at t=0.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Physics', 'Oscillations', 4, '(i) A = 5 cm = 0.05 m; ω = 4π rad/s. (ii) v_max = Aω = 20π cm/s. (iii) a_max = Aω² = 80π² cm/s². (iv) 5 sin(π/3) = 5√3/2 ≈ 4.33 cm.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'A laser displacement sensor records vertical harmonic oscillation of a spring-loaded platform described by y(t) = 5 sin(4π t + π/3) cm.', '(i) A = 5 cm = 0.05 m; ω = 4π rad/s. (ii) v_max = Aω = 20π cm/s. (iii) a_max = Aω² = 80π² cm/s². (iv) 5 sin(π/3) = 5√3/2 ≈ 4.33 cm.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000009d', 'Speed of a transverse wave in a stretched string of tension T and linear mass density μ is:', '{"A":"v = √(μ/T)","B":"v = √(T/μ)","C":"v = Tμ","D":"v = T/μ"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Waves', 1, 'v = √(T/μ).', NULL, NULL, 'v = √(T/μ).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000009e', 'Laplace corrected Newton''s formula for speed of sound in air by assuming sound propagation is:', '{"A":"Isothermal","B":"Adiabatic (P = K V^(-γ), giving v = √(γP/ρ))","C":"Isobaric","D":"Isochoric"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Waves', 1, 'Rapid compressions & rarefactions prevent heat escape -> adiabatic.', NULL, NULL, 'Rapid compressions & rarefactions prevent heat escape -> adiabatic.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-00000000009f', 'Two sound waves of frequencies 256 Hz and 260 Hz produce how many beats per second?', '{"A":"2 beats/s","B":"4 beats/s","C":"8 beats/s","D":"516 beats/s"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Waves', 1, 'Beat frequency = |f₁ - f₂| = 4 Hz.', NULL, NULL, 'Beat frequency = |f₁ - f₂| = 4 Hz.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a0', 'Ratio of fundamental frequency of an open organ pipe to a closed organ pipe of the same length L is:', '{"A":"1 : 2","B":"2 : 1","C":"3 : 1","D":"1 : 1"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Waves', 1, 'f_open = v/(2L); f_closed = v/(4L) -> 2 : 1.', NULL, NULL, 'f_open = v/(2L); f_closed = v/(4L) -> 2 : 1.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a1', 'Distance between two consecutive nodes or two consecutive antinodes in a standing wave is:', '{"A":"λ","B":"λ/2","C":"λ/4","D":"2λ"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Waves', 1, 'Node-to-node = λ/2; node-to-antinode = λ/4.', NULL, NULL, 'Node-to-node = λ/2; node-to-antinode = λ/4.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a2', 'Assertion (A): A closed organ pipe produces only odd harmonics (1st, 3rd, 5th...), whereas an open pipe produces all harmonics.
Reason (R): Closed end enforces a displacement node while open end enforces an antinode, allowing only odd quarter-wavelengths inside length L.
Choose the correct option:
A) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.
B) Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A.
C) A is true but R is false.
D) A is false but R is true.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, and R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Physics', 'Waves', 1, 'Boundary conditions exclude even harmonics in closed pipe.', NULL, NULL, 'Boundary conditions exclude even harmonics in closed pipe.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a3', 'A steel wire 0.72 m long has mass 5.0 × 10⁻³ kg. Under tension 60 N, calculate speed of transverse waves on the wire.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Waves', 2, 'μ = 5×10⁻³ / 0.72 = 6.944×10⁻³ kg/m. v = √(60 / 6.944×10⁻³) = √8640 ≈ 93 m/s.', 'μ calculation (1M) + ~93 m/s (1M)', NULL, 'μ = 5×10⁻³ / 0.72 = 6.944×10⁻³ kg/m. v = √(60 / 6.944×10⁻³) = √8640 ≈ 93 m/s.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a4', 'Distinguish between Progressive (travelling) waves and Stationary (standing) waves (two points).', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Waves', 2, 'Progressive waves transport energy outward and every particle attains equal amplitude; Standing waves trap energy between nodes (zero amplitude) and antinodes (maximum amplitude).', '2 points × 1M', NULL, 'Progressive waves transport energy outward and every particle attains equal amplitude; Standing waves trap energy between nodes (zero amplitude) and antinodes (maximum amplitude).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a5', 'Why did Newton''s isothermal formula underestimate the speed of sound in air (280 m/s vs 331 m/s)? Explain Laplace''s correction cleanly.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Waves', 3, 'Newton assumed slow isothermal pressure variations giving v = √(P/ρ) ≈ 280 m/s. Laplace showed air is a poor conductor and pressure oscillations are rapid (~kHz), making compressions/rarefactions adiabatic: v = √(γP/ρ) = √(1.4)×280 ≈ 331.3 m/s.', 'Newton flaw (1M) + Laplace adiabatic formula & 331 m/s (2M)', NULL, 'Newton assumed slow isothermal pressure variations giving v = √(P/ρ) ≈ 280 m/s. Laplace showed air is a poor conductor and pressure oscillations are rapid (~kHz), making compressions/rarefactions adiabatic: v = √(γP/ρ) = √(1.4)×280 ≈ 331.3 m/s.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a6', 'Prove mathematically that two tuning forks of slightly different frequencies ν₁ and ν₂ produce beat frequency ν_beat = |ν₁ - ν₂|.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Waves', 3, 'y₁ = a sin(2πν₁t), y₂ = a sin(2πν₂t) => y = [2a cos π(ν₁-ν₂)t] sin π(ν₁+ν₂)t. Intensity peaks twice per cosine cycle, giving max-intensity beat frequency = |ν₁ - ν₂|.', 'Superposition (2M) + |ν₁-ν₂| conclusion (1M)', NULL, 'y₁ = a sin(2πν₁t), y₂ = a sin(2πν₂t) => y = [2a cos π(ν₁-ν₂)t] sin π(ν₁+ν₂)t. Intensity peaks twice per cosine cycle, giving max-intensity beat frequency = |ν₁ - ν₂|.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a7', 'Derive the equation of a standing wave formed by superposition of two identical waves travelling in opposite directions: y = 2A sin(kx) cos(ωt). Find positions of Nodes and Antinodes, and sketch first three modes of vibration on a stretched string fixed at both ends.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Physics', 'Waves', 5, 'Superposition y = A sin(kx-ωt) + A sin(kx+ωt) = 2A sin(kx) cos(ωt). Nodes when sin(kx)=0 => x = nλ/2; Antinodes at (2n+1)λ/4 (3M). Modes on string: f_n = n v/(2L) -> ratios 1 : 2 : 3 (2M).', 'Derivation & nodes/antinodes (3M) + Modes of string (2M)', NULL, 'Superposition y = A sin(kx-ωt) + A sin(kx+ωt) = 2A sin(kx) cos(ωt). Nodes when sin(kx)=0 => x = nλ/2; Antinodes at (2n+1)λ/4 (3M). Modes on string: f_n = n v/(2L) -> ratios 1 : 2 : 3 (2M).', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a8', '(i) Amplitude, wavelength λ, and frequency f for y(x,t) = 3.0 sin(36t + 0.018x + π/4) cm (x in cm, t in s). (ii) Direction of wave travel. (iii) Speed of the wave. (iv) Least distance between two points with phase difference π/2.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Physics', 'Waves', 4, '(i) A=3 cm; k=0.018 cm⁻¹ => λ=2π/0.018 ≈ 349 cm (3.49 m); ω=36 => f=36/(2π) ≈ 5.73 Hz. (ii) Negative x-direction (+0.018x sign). (iii) v = ω/k = 36/0.018 = 2000 cm/s = 20 m/s. (iv) Δx = (λ/2π)(π/2) = λ/4 ≈ 87.2 cm.', '4 × 1M = 4M', 'A lab oscilloscope traces a progressive harmonic wave along a taut cable given by y(x,t) = 3.0 sin(36t + 0.018x + π/4) where x, y are in cm and t in seconds.', '(i) A=3 cm; k=0.018 cm⁻¹ => λ=2π/0.018 ≈ 349 cm (3.49 m); ω=36 => f=36/(2π) ≈ 5.73 Hz. (ii) Negative x-direction (+0.018x sign). (iii) v = ω/k = 36/0.018 = 2000 cm/s = 20 m/s. (iv) Δx = (λ/2π)(π/2) = λ/4 ≈ 87.2 cm.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a9', 'Which of the following pairs of physical quantities does NOT have identical dimensional formulas?', '{"A":"Work and Torque","B":"Angular momentum and Planck''s constant","C":"Tension and Surface Tension","D":"Impulse and Linear Momentum"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Units & Measurements', 1, 'Option (C) is correct. Tension is a force [M L T⁻²], whereas Surface Tension is force per unit length [M T⁻²]. All other pairs share identical dimensions.', 'Contrast Tension [MLT⁻²] vs Surface Tension [MT⁻²] [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Check dimensions of every pair carefully: Work/Torque = [ML²T⁻²], h/L = [ML²T⁻¹], Impulse/Momentum = [MLT⁻¹]. | Common Pitfall: Assuming Tension and Surface Tension both have dimensions of Force.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000aa', 'The number of significant figures in 0.002050 × 10⁴ is:', '{"A":"3","B":"4","C":"6","D":"7"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Units & Measurements', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. Leading zeros to the left of the first non-zero digit (0.00) are not significant; trapped zeros and trailing zeros after decimal (2050) are significant -> 4 significant figures. Powers of 10 do not alter significant figures.', 'Count digits 2, 0, 5, 0 -> 4 significant figures [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Trailing zero after a decimal point is ALWAYS significant. | Common Pitfall: Dropping the final zero or counting leading zeros.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000ab', 'A particle starts from rest with uniform acceleration a. The ratio of distances travelled in the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd seconds is:', '{"A":"1 : 2 : 3","B":"1 : 4 : 9","C":"1 : 3 : 5","D":"1 : 1 : 1"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Straight Line', 1, 'Option (C) is correct. By Galileo''s law of odd numbers, Sₙ = u + (a/2)(2n - 1). With u = 0, S₁ : S₂ : S₃ = 1 : 3 : 5.', 'Apply Sₙ ∝ (2n - 1) for u = 0 -> 1:3:5 [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Distinguish distance in "n seconds" (∝ n²) vs distance in "nth second" (∝ 2n-1). | Common Pitfall: Marking 1:4:9 (total distance after n seconds).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000ac', 'For a projectile launched at angle θ with horizontal, at what angle of projection is its maximum vertical height equal to its horizontal range?', '{"A":"θ = tan⁻¹(1)","B":"θ = tan⁻¹(2)","C":"θ = tan⁻¹(4)","D":"θ = tan⁻¹(1/4)"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Plane', 1, 'Option (C) is correct. H = R => (u² sin²θ)/(2g) = (u² sin 2θ)/g => (sin²θ)/2 = 2 sinθ cosθ => tan θ = 4 => θ = tan⁻¹(4).', 'Equate H = R -> sin²θ/2 = 2sinθcosθ -> tanθ = 4 [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Useful general identity: R = nH gives tan θ = 4/n. Here n = 1 => tan θ = 4. | Common Pitfall: Forgetting the factor of 2 in sin 2θ = 2 sinθ cosθ.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000ad', 'A person of mass 60 kg stands on a weighing scale inside a lift accelerating downwards at 2 m/s² (take g = 10 m/s²). The reading on the scale is:', '{"A":"720 N","B":"600 N","C":"480 N","D":"120 N"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Laws of Motion', 1, 'Option (C) is correct. Normal reaction R = m(g - a) = 60(10 - 2) = 480 N.', 'R = m(g - a) = 60 × 8 = 480 N [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Accelerating UP: m(g + a); Accelerating DOWN: m(g - a); Free fall: 0. | Common Pitfall: Adding a instead of subtracting for downward acceleration.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000ae', 'If the linear momentum of a body increases by 50%, its kinetic energy increases by:', '{"A":"50%","B":"100%","C":"125%","D":"225%"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Work, Energy & Power', 1, 'Option (C) is correct. K = p²/(2m). New p'' = 1.5 p => K'' = (1.5)² K = 2.25 K. Percentage increase = (2.25 - 1) × 100% = 125%.', 'K'' = (1.5)²K = 2.25K -> increase 125% [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Never use ΔK/K = 2(Δp/p) for large changes like 50% — that formula is only for small (<5%) changes! | Common Pitfall: Using linear approximation 2 × 50% = 100%.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000af', 'A solid sphere, a solid cylinder, and a circular ring of identical mass M and radius R roll down an inclined plane without slipping. Which reaches the bottom first?', '{"A":"Circular ring","B":"Solid cylinder","C":"Solid sphere","D":"All reach simultaneously"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Rotational Motion', 1, 'Option (C) is correct. Rolling acceleration a = g sinθ / (1 + I/(MR²)). Solid sphere has the smallest I/(MR²) = 2/5 = 0.4 (vs cylinder 0.5, ring 1.0), giving the largest acceleration.', 'Smallest I/(MR²) = 2/5 -> max acceleration -> Solid sphere first [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Smaller the k²/R² factor, faster the object rolls down. | Common Pitfall: Assuming all masses reach together like frictionless sliding.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b0', 'The escape velocity from Earth''s surface is vₑ ≈ 11.2 km/s. What would be the escape velocity from a planet having twice the mass and twice the radius of Earth?', '{"A":"5.6 km/s","B":"11.2 km/s","C":"22.4 km/s","D":"11.2√2 km/s"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Gravitation', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. vₑ = √(2GM/R). When both M and R double, M/R stays unchanged, so vₑ remains 11.2 km/s.', 'vₑ ∝ √(M/R) remains unchanged = 11.2 km/s [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Write the proportionality vₑ ∝ √(M/R) before substituting ratios. | Common Pitfall: Forgetting the square root or thinking radius doubles vₑ.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b1', 'A steel wire of length L and radius r can sustain a maximum load W without breaking. If the wire is cut into two equal halves, the maximum load each half can sustain is:', '{"A":"W / 2","B":"W","C":"2W","D":"W / 4"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Solids', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. Breaking load = Breaking Stress × Cross-sectional Area (πr²). Cutting length does not change area or material breaking stress, so max load remains W.', 'Breaking load depends on Area & Material, independent of length [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Young''s modulus and breaking stress are intensive material properties. | Common Pitfall: Thinking halving length doubles or halves breaking force.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b2', 'According to Stokes'' law, the terminal velocity vₜ of a small sphere falling through a viscous fluid is proportional to its radius r as:', '{"A":"r","B":"r²","C":"1/r","D":"1/r²"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Mechanical Properties of Fluids', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. vₜ = (2r²(ρ - σ)g) / (9η) => vₜ ∝ r².', 'State Stokes terminal velocity vₜ ∝ r² [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Eight tiny droplets merging into one big drop (R = 2r) increases terminal speed 4 times. | Common Pitfall: Marking proportional to r.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b3', 'Wien''s displacement law relates the wavelength λₘ of maximum spectral emission from a blackbody to its absolute temperature T as:', '{"A":"λₘ / T = constant","B":"λₘ T = constant","C":"λₘ T² = constant","D":"λₘ T⁴ = constant"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Thermal Properties of Matter', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. Wien''s law states λₘ T = b (Wien''s constant ≈ 2.9 × 10⁻³ m·K). Hotter stars emit shorter wavelengths (blue).', 'Identify λₘ T = constant [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Do not mix Wien''s law (λₘ T = b) with Stefan-Boltzmann law (E ∝ T⁴). | Common Pitfall: Selecting T⁴.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b4', 'In an adiabatic expansion of an ideal gas, which quantity remains strictly constant?', '{"A":"Temperature T","B":"Pressure P","C":"PV^γ","D":"Internal energy U"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Thermodynamics', 1, 'Option (C) is correct. For a quasi-static adiabatic process (δQ = 0), PV^γ = constant, where γ = Cp/Cv.', 'Identify PV^γ = constant for adiabatic process [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Isothermal: PV = const; Adiabatic: PV^γ = const. | Common Pitfall: Thinking temperature remains constant in adiabatic expansion (T actually drops!).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b5', 'Assertion (A): A physical equation that is dimensionally correct may still be physically incorrect.
Reason (R): Dimensional analysis cannot determine dimensionless numerical constants like 1/2 or 2π.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Physics', 'Units & Measurements', 1, 'Option (A) is correct. For example, K = mv² is dimensionally identical to K = ½mv² yet physically wrong because dimensions do not capture pure numerical constants.', 'Option (A) citing dimensionless constant limitation [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Dimensionally inconsistent => definitely wrong; Dimensionally consistent => may or may not be right. | Common Pitfall: Thinking dimensional validity proves complete correctness.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b6', 'Assertion (A): Action and reaction forces in Newton''s third law never cancel each other out.
Reason (R): Action and reaction act on two different bodies simultaneously.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Physics', 'Laws of Motion', 1, 'Option (A) is correct. Only forces acting on the SAME body can cancel to yield zero net force; third-law pairs act on two distinct interacting bodies.', 'Option (A) noting action-reaction act on different bodies [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Always check whether force pairs act on one body or two bodies. | Common Pitfall: Marking (C) by confusing normal force + weight (both on same block!) with third-law pair.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b7', 'Assertion (A): An ice skater spins faster when she folds her arms close to her body.
Reason (R): Folding her arms decreases her moment of inertia I, so angular speed ω increases to conserve angular momentum L = Iω.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Physics', 'Rotational Motion', 1, 'Option (A) is correct. With zero external torque, L = Iω = constant => reducing I automatically increases ω.', 'Option (A) applying conservation of angular momentum [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Mention zero external torque explicitly when explaining L = Iω. | Common Pitfall: Thinking rotational kinetic energy stays constant (it actually increases via internal muscle work!).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b8', 'Assertion (A): Specific heat of a gas in an isothermal process is infinity.
Reason (R): In an isothermal process, heat supplied causes no change in temperature (ΔT = 0), so C = Q/(mΔT) → ∞.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'A', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Physics', 'Thermodynamics', 1, 'Option (A) is correct. For isothermal ΔT = 0 with finite heat exchange Q, C = Q/(mΔT) = ∞; for adiabatic Q = 0 with ΔT ≠ 0, C = 0.', 'Option (A) using C = Q/(mΔT) [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Isothermal C = ∞; Adiabatic C = 0. | Common Pitfall: Mixing up isothermal (C=∞) with adiabatic (C=0).', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000b9', 'In the van der Waals equation (P + a/V²)(V - b) = RT, find the dimensional formulas of constants "a" and "b", where P is pressure and V is volume.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Units & Measurements', 2, 'By principle of homogeneity of dimensions:
1. [a / V²] = [P] => [a] = [P][V²] = [M L⁻¹ T⁻²] × [L³]² = [M L⁵ T⁻²].
2. [b] = [V] = [L³].', 'Apply principle of homogeneity to get [a] = [M L⁵ T⁻²] [1 Mark] | Find [b] = [L³] [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Only quantities with identical dimensions can be added or subtracted. | Common Pitfall: Taking V as velocity [LT⁻¹] instead of volume [L³].', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000ba', 'Two vectors A and B satisfy |A + B| = |A - B|. Find the angle between A and B.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Plane', 2, 'Squaring both sides:
|A + B|² = |A - B|²
A² + B² + 2AB cosθ = A² + B² - 2AB cosθ
4AB cosθ = 0 => cosθ = 0 (since A, B ≠ 0) => θ = 90°.', 'Expand magnitudes squared [1 Mark] | Solve 4AB cosθ = 0 -> θ = 90° [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Geometrically, diagonals of a parallelogram are equal only when it is a rectangle (θ = 90°). | Common Pitfall: Forgetting to state A ≠ 0, B ≠ 0.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000bb', 'A bullet of mass 20 g is fired horizontally with velocity 150 m/s from a pistol of mass 2 kg. Calculate the recoil velocity of the pistol.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Laws of Motion', 2, 'By conservation of linear momentum (initial momentum = 0):
m₁v₁ + m₂v₂ = 0
(0.02 kg)(150 m/s) + (2 kg) v₂ = 0
3 + 2v₂ = 0 => v₂ = -1.5 m/s.
Recoil speed is 1.5 m/s opposite to bullet direction.', 'Convert 20 g = 0.02 kg & apply m₁v₁ + m₂v₂ = 0 [1 Mark] | v₂ = -1.5 m/s [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Always convert mass into SI unit kg! | Common Pitfall: Using m₁ = 20 g alongside m₂ = 2 kg without conversion.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000bc', 'At what depth below the surface of Earth does the acceleration due to gravity become 25% of its value on the surface? (Take R = 6400 km)', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Gravitation', 2, 'Variation with depth: g_d = g (1 - d/R).
Given g_d = 0.25 g = g/4 => 1 - d/R = 1/4 => d/R = 3/4.
d = (3/4) × 6400 km = 4800 km.', 'Formula g_d = g(1 - d/R) [1 Mark] | Compute d = 4800 km [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Read carefully: "becomes 25%" vs "decreases by 25%"! | Common Pitfall: Using height formula g/(1 + h/R)² for depth.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000bd', 'The displacement of a particle executing SHM is given by x(t) = 0.04 sin(100t + π/3) metres. Find its amplitude, angular frequency, frequency, and maximum acceleration.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Oscillations', 2, 'Comparing with x = A sin(ωt + φ):
Amplitude A = 0.04 m; Angular frequency ω = 100 rad/s.
Frequency ν = ω / (2π) = 100 / (2π) = 50/π ≈ 15.92 Hz.
Max acceleration a_max = ω² A = (100)² × 0.04 = 10000 × 0.04 = 400 m/s².', 'A = 0.04 m, ω = 100 rad/s, ν = 50/π Hz [1 Mark] | a_max = ω²A = 400 m/s² [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: State SI units (m, rad/s, Hz, m/s²) for each quantity. | Common Pitfall: Confusing ω with frequency ν = ω/2π.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000be', 'Assuming that the time period T of a simple pendulum depends on mass of bob m, length l, and acceleration due to gravity g, derive the expression T = k √(l/g) using dimensional analysis.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Units & Measurements', 3, 'Let T ∝ mᵃ lᵇ gᶜ => T = k mᵃ lᵇ gᶜ.
Writing dimensions: [M⁰ L⁰ T¹] = [M]ᵃ [L]ᵇ [L T⁻²]ᶜ = [Mᵃ L^(b+c) T^(-2c)].
Equating exponents:
• a = 0
• -2c = 1 => c = -1/2
• b + c = 0 => b = -c = +1/2.
Substituting back: T = k m⁰ l^(1/2) g^(-1/2) = k √(l/g).', 'Dimensional substitution [Mᵃ L^(b+c) T^(-2c)] [1 Mark] | Solve a=0, b=1/2, c=-1/2 [1 Mark] | Final formula T = k√(l/g) [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Keep dimensionless constant k as dimensions cannot find 2π. | Common Pitfall: Forgetting to write k (writing = instead of k).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000bf', 'Using integration (calculus method), derive all three kinematic equations of motion for constant acceleration a:
(i) v = u + at
(ii) s = ut + ½at²
(iii) v² - u² = 2as', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Straight Line', 3, '(i) a = dv/dt => dv = a dt. Integrating from u to v and 0 to t: ∫dv = a∫dt => v - u = at => v = u + at.
(ii) v = ds/dt => ds = (u + at)dt. Integrating s from 0 to s and t from 0 to t: s = ut + ½at².
(iii) a = (dv/ds)(ds/dt) = v dv/ds => v dv = a ds. Integrating v from u to v and s from 0 to s: [v²/2] = a[s] => v² - u² = 2as.', '(i) Derive v = u + at via integration [1 Mark] | (ii) Derive s = ut + ½at² [1 Mark] | (iii) Use chain rule a = v dv/ds -> v² - u² = 2as [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Always specify lower and upper limits of integration clearly. | Common Pitfall: Using indefinite integration without finding constants.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000c0', 'A particle of mass m tied to a string of length L is rotated in a vertical circle. Derive expressions for the minimum velocity at:
(a) Highest point
(b) Lowest point
just to complete the vertical loop.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Work, Energy & Power', 3, '(a) At highest point H, gravity alone supplies centripetal force when T_top = 0:
mg = m v_H² / L => v_H = √(gL).
(b) By conservation of mechanical energy between lowest point L_0 and highest point H (height difference = 2L):
½ m v_L² = ½ m v_H² + mg(2L) = ½ m (gL) + 2mgL = (5/2) mgL
=> v_L = √(5gL).', 'Set T_top = 0 to find v_H = √(gL) [1.5 Marks] | Apply mechanical energy conservation over Δh = 2L -> v_L = √(5gL) [1.5 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Tension difference between bottom and top is always T_L - T_H = 6mg! | Common Pitfall: Taking height difference between top and bottom as L instead of 2L.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000c1', 'Define torque and angular momentum. Derive the relation τ = dL/dt for a particle and show how angular momentum is conserved when net external torque is zero.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Rotational Motion', 3, 'Torque τ = r × F and Angular Momentum L = r × p.
Differentiating L w.r.t. time t:
dL/dt = d/dt(r × p) = (dr/dt × p) + (r × dp/dt).
Since dr/dt = v and p = mv, v × (mv) = 0.
And by Newton''s second law, dp/dt = F_ext => dL/dt = r × F_ext = τ_ext.
If net external torque τ_ext = 0, then dL/dt = 0 => L = constant (Law of Conservation of Angular Momentum).', 'Definitions τ = r × F and L = r × p [1 Mark] | Product rule differentiation showing v × p = 0 [1.5 Marks] | τ = 0 => L = constant [0.5 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Maintain vector cross-product order r × p (never write p × r!). | Common Pitfall: Dropping vector cross-product notation.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000c2', 'Derive an expression for the work done during a quasi-static isothermal expansion of n moles of an ideal gas from volume V₁ to V₂ at constant temperature T.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Thermodynamics', 3, 'Small work done dW = P dV. For n moles of an ideal gas, PV = nRT => P = nRT / V.
Total work W = ∫_{V₁}^{V₂} (nRT / V) dV = nRT ∫_{V₁}^{V₂} (1/V) dV
= nRT [ln V]_{V₁}^{V₂} = nRT ln(V₂ / V₁) = 2.303 nRT log₁₀(V₂ / V₁).', 'Set up dW = P dV with P = nRT/V [1 Mark] | Integrate 1/V between V₁ and V₂ [1.5 Marks] | Convert ln to log₁₀: 2.303 nRT log₁₀(V₂/V₁) [0.5 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: In terms of pressures (P₁V₁ = P₂V₂), W = 2.303 nRT log₁₀(P₁/P₂). | Common Pitfall: Writing log(V₁/V₂) instead of log(V₂/V₁).', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000c3', 'Draw a typical stress-strain curve for a ductile metallic wire and clearly label: (i) Proportional limit, (ii) Yield point (elastic limit), (iii) Ultimate tensile strength, and (iv) Fracture point.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Thermal Properties', 3, '• O to A (Proportional Limit): Hooke''s law holds strictly (stress ∝ strain; linear graph; slope = Young''s modulus Y).
• A to B (Elastic Limit / Yield Point): Body returns to original dimensions when load removed; B is yield point beyond which plastic deformation begins.
• D (Ultimate Tensile Strength): Maximum stress the material can withstand before necking starts.
• E (Fracture Point): Wire breaks even under slightly reduced stress beyond D.', 'Neat stress vs strain plot with all 4 points marked [1.5 Marks] | Physical meaning of Proportional limit, Yield, UTS & Fracture [1.5 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Recall Stress is plotted on Y-axis and Strain on X-axis (so slope = Y). | Common Pitfall: Placing Strain on Y-axis instead of X-axis.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000c4', 'Why did Newton''s formula v = √(P/ρ) give an incorrect value for the speed of sound in air? Explain Laplace''s correction and compute the corrected formula.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Waves', 3, 'Newton assumed sound propagation in air is an ISOTHERMAL process (B_iso = P), giving v ≈ 280 m/s at STP vs experimental 331 m/s (15% error).
Laplace showed that compressions and rarefactions occur too rapidly in air (a poor conductor of heat) for heat exchange to happen, making the process ADIABATIC.
Thus adiabatic bulk modulus B_ad = γP => v = √(γP/ρ). For air (γ = 1.4), v = √(1.4) × 280 ≈ 331.3 m/s, matching experiment.', 'Newton''s isothermal assumption -> 280 m/s discrepancy [1 Mark] | Laplace''s adiabatic reasoning (rapid + poor conductor) [1 Mark] | Corrected formula v = √(γP/ρ) ≈ 331.3 m/s [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Highlight γ = Cp/Cv = 1.4 for diatomic gases like N₂/O₂ in air. | Common Pitfall: Confusing isothermal vs adiabatic bulk modulus.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000c5', 'Read the passage and answer:
To reduce wear and tear on car tyres and avoid skidding at high speeds, the outer edge of curved highways is raised slightly above the inner edge by an angle θ (banking of roads).

(a) On a flat circular road of radius R and coefficient of friction μ, write the expression for maximum safe speed. (1M)
(b) Derive or state the optimum speed on a frictionless banked road of angle θ and radius R (zero wear & tear). (1M)
(c) A circular track of radius 300 m is banked at angle θ such that tan θ = 0.15. Find the optimum speed (g = 10 m/s²). Also state the role of centripetal force direction. (2M)', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Physics', 'Case Study', 4, '(a) Flat road: v_max = √(μ R g).
(b) Optimum speed (zero friction needed): v₀ = √(R g tan θ).
(c) v₀ = √(300 × 10 × 0.15) = √450 ≈ 21.21 m/s. Horizontal component of Normal reaction (N sinθ) provides the inward centripetal force towards the centre of the circular track.', '(a) √(μRg) [1 Mark] | (b) √(Rg tanθ) [1 Mark] | (c) v₀ = √450 ≈ 21.21 m/s + N sinθ explanation [2 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Optimum speed v₀ requires ZERO friction because N sinθ alone balances mv²/R! | Common Pitfall: Confusing tan θ with sin θ in v₀ formula.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000c6', 'Read the passage and answer:
Blood flows through an artery of cross-sectional area 8 × 10⁻⁶ m² at an average speed of 0.4 m/s. When plaque narrows a segment (stenosis) to half its cross-sectional area, pressure drops significantly inside the constriction.

(a) State the Equation of Continuity for incompressible fluid flow. (1M)
(b) Find the blood speed inside the narrowed plaque region. (1M)
(c) Using Bernoulli''s principle, explain why severe narrowing can cause temporary collapse of an artery. (2M)', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Physics', 'Case Study', 4, '(a) A₁v₁ = A₂v₂ (volume flow rate Av = constant for steady incompressible streamline flow).
(b) A₂ = A₁/2 => v₂ = 2 v₁ = 2 × 0.4 = 0.8 m/s.
(c) By Bernoulli''s theorem (P + ½ρv² + ρgh = constant), higher speed v₂ in the constriction lowers internal fluid pressure P₂ dramatically. External tissue pressure can exceed P₂ and temporarily collapse the artery.', '(a) A₁v₁ = A₂v₂ [1 Mark] | (b) v₂ = 0.8 m/s [1 Mark] | (c) Bernoulli pressure drop causing external collapse [2 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Equation of continuity is direct conservation of mass. | Common Pitfall: Saying pressure increases when area narrows.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000c7', 'A projectile is fired with initial velocity u at an angle θ with the horizontal.
(a) Show that the path of the projectile is a parabola.
(b) Derive expressions for Time of Flight (T), Maximum Height (H), and Horizontal Range (R).
(c) Prove that there are two angles of projection for the same horizontal range with identical initial speed.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Physics', 'Motion in a Plane', 5, '(a) x = (u cosθ)t => t = x/(u cosθ). Vertical displacement y = (u sinθ)t - ½gt² = x tanθ - [g / (2u² cos²θ)] x². Since y = ax - bx² is quadratic in x, the trajectory is a parabola.
(b) At max height v_y = 0 => 0 = u sinθ - gt_asc => T = 2t_asc = (2u sinθ)/g.
H = (u² sin²θ)/(2g).
R = (u cosθ) × T = (u² sin 2θ)/g.
(c) Replacing θ by (90° - θ): R'' = u² sin(180° - 2θ)/g = u² sin 2θ / g = R. Thus θ and (90° - θ) yield identical range.', '(a) Parabolic equation y = x tanθ - gx²/(2u²cos²θ) [2 Marks] | (b) Derivations of T, H, R [2 Marks] | (c) Complementary angles θ and 90°-θ proof [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Draw a clean projectile arc labelling u_x = u cosθ, u_y = u sinθ, H and R. | Common Pitfall: Writing sin²θ instead of sin 2θ in Range formula.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000c8', '(a) Draw a free-body diagram of a car of mass m negotiating a circular banked road of radius R banked at angle θ with coefficient of static friction μ_s.
(b) Derive the exact expression for maximum safe permissible speed v_max.
(c) A cyclist speeds at 18 km/h on a level circular turn of radius 9 m with μ_s = 0.3 (g = 10 m/s²). Will the cyclist slip?', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Physics', 'Laws of Motion', 5, '(a) Forces on car: Weight mg downward, Normal N perpendicular to banked road, Static friction f_s down the incline (for max speed).
(b) Vertical equilibrium: N cosθ - f_s sinθ = mg. Horizontal centripetal: N sinθ + f_s cosθ = mv_max²/R. Putting f_s = μ_s N and dividing:
v_max = √[ R g (μ_s + tan θ) / (1 - μ_s tan θ) ].
(c) v = 18 km/h = 5 m/s. Max safe speed on level road = √(μ_s R g) = √(0.3 × 9 × 10) = √27 ≈ 5.196 m/s. Since 5 m/s < 5.196 m/s, the cyclist will NOT slip.', '(a) FBD resolving N and f_s along vertical & horizontal [1.5 Marks] | (b) Derivation of v_max [2 Marks] | (c) Convert 18 km/h = 5 m/s < √27 m/s -> No slip [1.5 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Always convert km/h to m/s by multiplying by 5/18. | Common Pitfall: Resolving friction parallel to horizontal instead of along the inclined road.', 'HARD', true)
        ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

Derivation via Work-Energy theorem on fluid element of mass Δm = ρΔV passing from (A₁, v₁, P₁, h₁) to (A₂, v₂, P₂, h₂): Net work (P₁ - P₂)ΔV = ΔK + ΔU = ½ρΔV(v₂² - v₁²) + ρΔVg(h₂ - h₁). Dividing by ΔV gives P₁ + ½ρv₁² + ρgh₁ = P₂ + ½ρv₂² + ρgh₂.
(b) Applications: Atomiser/sprayer, aerofoil lift on aircraft wings, Venturimeter.
(c) Speed of efflux v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 10 × 5) = √100 = 10 m/s.', '(a) Statement + Work-Energy proof [3 Marks] | (b) Two applications [1 Mark] | (c) Torricelli speed v = 10 m/s [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: State the four ideal-fluid assumptions: steady, incompressible, non-viscous, irrotational. | Common Pitfall: Forgetting potential energy term ρgh in derivation.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000ca', 'At room temperature, the average translational kinetic energy per molecule of an ideal gas depends only on:', '{"A":"Molar mass of gas","B":"Absolute temperature T","C":"Volume of container","D":"Pressure alone"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Kinetic Theory of Gases', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. Mean translational KE per molecule = (3/2) k_B T, which depends solely on absolute temperature T.', 'KE = (3/2) k_B T depends only on T [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: All ideal gases at the same temperature have the SAME average translational KE per molecule, regardless of mass. | Common Pitfall: Thinking heavier molecules have higher kinetic energy.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000cb', 'A simple pendulum of time period T is taken inside an artificial satellite orbiting the Earth. Its time period inside the satellite will be:', '{"A":"T","B":"T / 2","C":"Zero","D":"Infinite (does not oscillate)"}'::jsonb, 'D', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Oscillations', 1, 'Option (D) is correct. Inside an orbiting satellite, effective gravity g_eff = 0 (weightlessness). Since T = 2π√(L/g_eff), T → ∞.', 'g_eff = 0 in satellite -> T = ∞ [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: A spring-mass system T = 2π√(m/k) is unaffected by g and oscillates normally in space! | Common Pitfall: Writing T = 0 when g_eff = 0.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000cc', 'Assertion (A): When a fast train passes a stationary person standing close to the track, the person tends to be pulled towards the train.
Reason (R): Fast-moving air between the train and person creates a region of higher pressure compared to outside.', '{"A":"Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true, but R is false","D":"A is false, but R is true"}'::jsonb, 'C', 'ASSERTION_REASON', 'Physics', 'Fluids', 1, 'Option (C) is correct. By Bernoulli''s theorem, fast-moving air creates LOWER pressure (not higher pressure) between train and person, so outside higher pressure pushes the person inward.', 'Option (C): A true, R false (higher velocity -> lower pressure) [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Read Reason carefully: high speed => LOW static pressure. | Common Pitfall: Assuming fast air creates high static pressure.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000cd', 'Why is the excess pressure inside a soap bubble twice that inside an air bubble of the same radius in a liquid? Write both formulas.', NULL, NULL, 'VSA', 'Physics', 'Properties of Fluids', 2, 'An air bubble inside a liquid has only ONE free spherical surface interface (liquid-air), so ΔP_air = 2S / R.
A soap bubble in air has TWO free surfaces (inner and outer air-soap interfaces), doubling surface energy change, so ΔP_soap = 4S / R.', 'Air bubble = 1 free surface (2S/R) [1 Mark] | Soap bubble = 2 free surfaces (4S/R) [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Explicitly state "two free surfaces" for soap bubble. | Common Pitfall: Saying soap has double surface tension instead of two interfaces.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000ce', 'Write the kinetic theory formula for pressure P exerted by an ideal gas and use it to calculate the RMS speed of nitrogen molecules (M = 28 g/mol) at 27°C (R = 8.314 J/mol·K).', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'Physics', 'Kinetic Theory', 3, 'Pressure P = (1/3) ρ v_rms² = (1/3) (nM/V) v_rms² => v_rms = √(3RT / M).
Given T = 27 + 273 = 300 K, M = 28 × 10⁻³ kg/mol.
v_rms = √(3 × 8.314 × 300 / 0.028) = √(7482.6 / 0.028) = √267235.7 ≈ 517 m/s.', 'Formula P = (1/3)ρv_rms² & v_rms = √(3RT/M) [1.5 Marks] | Convert T = 300 K and M = 0.028 kg/mol [0.5 Mark] | Final RMS speed ≈ 517 m/s [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Convert BOTH Temperature (to Kelvin) and Molar Mass (to kg/mol)! | Common Pitfall: Substituting M = 28 instead of 0.028 kg/mol.', 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000cf', 'Read the passage and answer:
Communication and Earth-observation satellites orbit at fixed altitudes balanced between gravitational attraction and centripetal requirement. A satellite of mass 500 kg orbits Earth at height h = R (where Earth radius R = 6.4 × 10⁶ m, g = 10 m/s²).

(a) State Kepler''s Third Law (Law of Periods). (1M)
(b) Write the formula for orbital speed of a satellite at height h above Earth''s surface. (1M)
(c) Calculate the orbital speed of the given satellite at h = R. (2M)', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'Physics', 'Case Study', 4, '(a) The square of the time period T of revolution of a planet/satellite is directly proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis r of its elliptical/circular orbit (T² ∝ r³).
(b) v_o = √(GM / (R + h)) = R √(g / (R + h)).
(c) At h = R, R + h = 2R => v_o = √(gR / 2) = √(10 × 6.4 × 10⁶ / 2) = √(3.2 × 10⁷) = 4000√2 ≈ 5.66 × 10³ m/s (5.66 km/s).', '(a) T² ∝ r³ statement [1 Mark] | (b) v_o = √(GM/(R+h)) [1 Mark] | (c) Substitute r = 2R -> 5.66 km/s [2 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Notice orbital speed is independent of satellite mass (500 kg is extra data)! | Common Pitfall: Using r = R instead of R + h = 2R.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000d0', '(a) Derive expressions for kinetic energy, potential energy, and total mechanical energy of a particle executing SHM of amplitude A and mass m. Plot them vs displacement x.
(b) Show that oscillations of a simple pendulum for small angular displacements are simple harmonic, and derive T = 2π√(L/g).', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Physics', 'Oscillations', 5, '(a) Displacement x = A sin(ωt), velocity v = ω√(A² - x²).
KE = ½ m ω² (A² - x²); PE = ½ m ω² x².
Total Energy E = KE + PE = ½ m ω² A² = ½ k A² (constant, independent of x). Graph: PE is upward parabola, KE is inverted parabola, Total E is horizontal line.
(b) Restoring torque τ = -mgL sinθ ≈ -mgLθ (for small θ). Using τ = Iα = (mL²) d²θ/dt² => d²θ/dt² = -(g/L)θ = -ω²θ.
Hence motion is SHM with ω = √(g/L) => T = 2π/ω = 2π√(L/g).', '(a) KE, PE, Total E expressions + parabolic graph [2.5 Marks] | (b) Restoring torque & small-angle sinθ≈θ -> T = 2π√(L/g) [2.5 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Explicitly state the small-angle approximation sin θ ≈ θ (in radians). | Common Pitfall: Forgetting to write why total energy graph is a straight horizontal line.', 'HARD', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000d1', 'In a closed organ pipe (closed at one end, open at the other), which harmonics are produced?', '{"A":"Only even harmonics","B":"Only odd harmonics (1st, 3rd, 5th...)","C":"All harmonics (even and odd)","D":"Only 2nd harmonic"}'::jsonb, 'B', 'MCQ', 'Physics', 'Waves', 1, 'Option (B) is correct. With a node at the closed end and antinode at the open end, frequencies are νₙ = (2n - 1)v / (4L) -> odd harmonics only (1 : 3 : 5).', 'Odd harmonics only (1:3:5) [1 Mark]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Open organ pipe produces ALL harmonics (1:2:3); closed organ pipe produces ONLY ODD harmonics (1:3:5). | Common Pitfall: Confusing open pipe with closed pipe.', 'EASY', true),
        ('11011111-0000-4000-8000-0000000000d2', '(a) Derive expressions for final velocities v₁ and v₂ after an elastic one-dimensional collision between two bodies of masses m₁ and m₂ moving with initial velocities u₁ and u₂.
(b) Discuss special cases: (i) m₁ = m₂, and (ii) a light body hitting a massive stationary wall (m₁ ≪ m₂, u₂ = 0).', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'Physics', 'Work, Energy & Power', 5, '(a) Conservation of momentum: m₁u₁ + m₂u₂ = m₁v₁ + m₂v₂ => m₁(u₁ - v₁) = m₂(v₂ - u₂) ...(1)
Conservation of KE: ½m₁u₁² + ½m₂u₂² = ½m₁v₁² + ½m₂v₂² => relative velocity u₁ - u₂ = v₂ - v₁ => v₂ = u₁ - u₂ + v₁ ...(2)
Solving (1) & (2):
v₁ = [(m₁ - m₂)/(m₁ + m₂)]u₁ + [2m₂/(m₁ + m₂)]u₂
v₂ = [2m₁/(m₁ + m₂)]u₁ + [(m₂ - m₁)/(m₁ + m₂)]u₂.
(b) (i) If m₁ = m₂: v₁ = u₂ and v₂ = u₁ (velocities interchange!).
(ii) If m₁ ≪ m₂, u₂ = 0: v₁ ≈ -u₁ (rebounds at same speed), v₂ ≈ 0.', 'Momentum + KE conservation -> relative speed reversal [1.5 Marks] | Expressions for v₁ and v₂ [2 Marks] | Both special cases clearly proved [1.5 Marks]', NULL, 'Examiner Tip: Using u₁ - u₂ = v₂ - v₁ makes solving simultaneous equations very fast! | Common Pitfall: Sign mistakes in (m₁ - m₂) vs (m₂ - m₁).', 'HARD', true)
